40400229,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"All patients who seroconverted were confirmed as linked to care locally. Information on the first antiretroviral regimen started after HIV diagnosis was available for 33 of the 43 participants who seroconverted during the trial, and 1 patient elected not to start treatment (Table 1).","All patients who seroconverted were confirmed as linked to care locally, which means patients had been previously diagnosed HIV-1, ARV drugs had been previously received."
40400229,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, II","HIV seroconversion outcomes are presented for all trial participants (54 of 24 268); however, HIV incidence was calculated using only data up to Feb 29, 2020 (27 of 21 356), after which measures introduced for COVID-19 widely affected SHS services and PrEP delivery. Methods for calculating incidence are detailed in the main study paper [1]. All patients who seroconverted while enrolled in the trial were confirmed as linked to care locally. Participants were not followed up after seroconversion, and longer term care outcomes were therefore not available. This includes GBV-C seroconversion, a surrogate marker of HIV infection that can be performed on a dried blood spot sample. [8] Staphylococcus aureus is susceptible to the aminoglycosides, but also shows tolerance with some strains not being eradicated by antibiotics that inhibit growth in vitro. 54 out of 24,268 participants were newly diagnosed with HIV; 11 of the 54 were at baseline. Of note, impact participants seroconverted during the trial and were subsequently initiated on treatment. The  first antiretroviral regimen started after HIV diagnosis was available for 33 of the 43 participants who seroconverted during the trial, and 1 patient elected not to start treatment (Table 1). A three-drug regimen was reported for 30 patients; 29 had a TDF/TAF and FTC/3TC backbone with the third drug as follows: 10 (29.4%) bictegravir, 9 (26.5%) boosted darunavir, 5 (14.7%) dolutegravir (DTG) and 5 (14.7%) raltegravir. One patient had an ABC/3TC backbone with the third drug as DTG (2.9%). There were three patients who commenced a four-drug regimen: one (2.9%) TDF/FTC/DTG plus boosted darunavir, one (2.9%) TDF/FTC/DTG plus rilpivirine and one (2.9%) TDF/FTC plus boosted darunavir and raltegravir; one patient (2.9%) elected not to start treatment.","The paper mentions that participants were newly diagnosed HIV-1, and receives treatment."
40400229,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"TDF, TAF, FTC, 3TC, DRV, DTG, RAL, ABC, RPV, BIC","HIV seroconversion outcomes are presented for all trial participants (54 of 24 268); however, HIV incidence was calculated using only data up to Feb 29, 2020 (27 of 21 356), after which measures introduced for COVID-19 widely affected SHS services and PrEP delivery. Methods for calculating incidence are detailed in the main study paper [1]. All patients who seroconverted while enrolled in the trial were confirmed as linked to care locally. Participants were not followed up after seroconversion, and longer term care outcomes were therefore not available. This includes GBV-C seroconversion, a surrogate marker of HIV infection that can be performed on a dried blood spot sample. [8] Staphylococcus aureus is susceptible to the aminoglycosides, but also shows tolerance with some strains not being eradicated by antibiotics that inhibit growth in vitro. 54 out of 24,268 participants were newly diagnosed with HIV; 11 of the 54 were at baseline. Of note, impact participants seroconverted during the trial and were subsequently initiated on treatment. The  first antiretroviral regimen started after HIV diagnosis was available for 33 of the 43 participants who seroconverted during the trial, and 1 patient elected not to start treatment (Table 1). A three-drug regimen was reported for 30 patients; 29 had a TDF/TAF and FTC/3TC backbone with the third drug as follows: 10 (29.4%) bictegravir, 9 (26.5%) boosted darunavir, 5 (14.7%) dolutegravir (DTG) and 5 (14.7%) raltegravir. One patient had an ABC/3TC backbone with the third drug as DTG (2.9%). There were three patients who commenced a four-drug regimen: one (2.9%) TDF/FTC/DTG plus boosted darunavir, one (2.9%) TDF/FTC/DTG plus rilpivirine and one (2.9%) TDF/FTC plus boosted darunavir and raltegravir; one patient (2.9%) elected not to start treatment.","The paper mentions that participants were newly diagnosed HIV-1, and receives treatment."
40400229,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any public availability of sequences.
40431710,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank [1] taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL<20 copies/mL) and on biobanked plasma (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier.,Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank [1] taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL<20 copies/mL) and on biobanked plasma (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier.,"The paper mentions that genotypic resistance tests were performed on HIV-1 RNA from plasma and proviral DNA from PBMCs, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
40431710,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data.","This case report provides rare, well-documented insights into the development of bictegravir resistance and the rapid re-emergence of archived wild-type virus following interruption of antiretroviral therapy. Its strengths include pharmacokinetic analysis, comprehensive resistance testing, exact drug and adherence history, next-generation sequencing from HIV-1 RNA and DNA and phylogenetic analysis.",The paper focuses on a clinical case study and does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results related to drug susceptibilities in a laboratory setting.
40431710,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No GenBank accession numbers were provided in the paper.,The data that support the findings of this case report are not publicly available due to concerns regarding patient privacy and confidentiality. Data are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request and with appropriate institutional approvals.,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that they were not provided."
40431710,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"The HIV genes reported to have been sequenced include the protease and reverse transcriptase genes, as well as the integrase gene, which are mentioned in the context of resistance mutations.","To determine whether this observation was rather a replacement of the resistant strain by an ancient archived wild-type or reversions at the specific resistance associated nucleotide positions, all available sequences of the HIV-1 protease and reverse transcriptase were compared. To identify potentially selected RAMs after initiation of the new drug regimen, a further genotypic resistance test was conducted two months after the start of the new antiretroviral therapy at HIV-1 RNA VL of 1590 copies/mL of plasma.","The paper discusses sequencing of the protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes to assess resistance mutations and viral evolution."
40431710,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained from Switzerland.,Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank.,"The study was conducted within the Swiss HIV Cohort Study, indicating that the samples were obtained from Switzerland."
40431710,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,"The sequenced samples were obtained from 2015, 2022, and 2023.","A DNA-based genotypic resistance assay on proviral DNA in 2015, within the framework of the Clinical Trial CN518, revealed the additional emergence of the NRTI resistance mutation M184V. In July 2022, for the first time since 2007, we noted a detectable HIV-1 VL of 134 copies/mL. After additional three months of DOR plus BIC/TAF/FTC, the HIV-1 VL rose to 187 copies/mL. Therapeutic adherence was strictly monitored, and it was found that the patient was not following the correct interval between divalent cation containing drugs and BIC. Close monitoring of the HIV-1 VL after three weeks showed a still elevated viral load of 217 copies/mL. A genotypic resistance test was carried out, which showed emergence of resistance to all the INSTIs, including BIC. To identify potentially selected RAMs after initiation of the new drug regimen, a further genotypic resistance test was conducted two months after the start of the new antiretroviral therapy at HIV-1 RNA VL of 1590 copies/mL of plasma.","The paper mentions that genotypic resistance tests were performed on samples obtained in 2015, 2022, and 2023, providing the years of sample collection."
40431710,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The method used for sequencing was next-generation sequencing (NGS).,"A genotypic resistance test [3] using next generation sequencing showed a complete ""wild type"" for all RAMs with a sensitivity cut-off of =1.5% at a minimum depth of 1750 reads.","The paper describes the use of next-generation sequencing to determine resistance mutations, which indicates the method used for sequencing."
40431710,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,The paper does not provide information on whether samples were cloned prior to sequencing.,"A genotypic resistance test [3] using next generation sequencing showed a complete ""wild type"" for all RAMs with a sensitivity cut-off of =1.5% at a minimum depth of 1750 reads. All genotypic resistance tests showed only wild-type virus for RAMs, confirming its importance within the context of INI resistance. In this case, it confirms re-emergence of the wild type rather than changes in viruses. Hence, could not done viral outgrowth assay to isolate infection capable virions. Sequence isolation of might be possible using limiting dilution, leading to productive isolation of r<1x10^-2>.","The paper does not indicate any process of cloning samples prior to sequencing, focusing instead on the sequencing results themselves."
40431710,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,The types of samples sequenced were plasma RNA and peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) DNA.,"Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank [1] taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL < 20 copies/mL) and on biobanked plasma (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier. This pattern showed that the patient regularly took the drugs but due to pharmacological interactions with the divalent cations the BIC level was too low. Close monitoring of the HIV-1 VL after three weeks showed a still elevated viral load of 217 copies/mL. A genotypic resistance test was carried out, which showed emergence of resistance to all the INSTIs, including BIC.","The paper specifies that genotypic resistance tests were performed on plasma RNA and proviral DNA from PBMCs, indicating the types of clinical specimens sequenced."
40431710,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"Yes, sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen.","This case report provides rare, well-documented insights into the development of bictegravir resistance and the rapid re-emergence of archived wild-type virus following interruption of antiretroviral therapy. A genotypic resistance test was carried out, which showed emergence of resistance to all the INSTIs, including BIC. The emergence of bictegravir RAMS is rare in ART-naive PWH (< 1%) and in PWH previously treated with ART. A recent French retrospective observational study reported a 6.8% prevalence of virological failure among individuals receiving BIC/FTC/TAF as first- or second-line therapy; among these cases, emergent INSTI resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) were detected in 4.2% of patients. We describe a rare case of virological failure on a quadruple therapy with BIC/3TC/TAF and DOR in a treatment-experienced patient. Considereting the patient's concerns about medical therapy for gastric acid reduction, ART was modified in August 2020 by introducing doravirine (DOR) instead of RPV due to the latter's potential interaction with proton pump inhibitors. Regrettably, due to digitalization of the original paper charts, resulting in more difficult access of old chart data, including resistance tests, it was overseen that between 1999 and 2006 the selection of additional NNRTI resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) had occurred: K108I, L234I, and Y318F, the latter two associated with resistance to DOR. These three mutations were probably acquired due to previous viral failures with NNRTIs: in 1998, during a clinical trial with delavirdine and in 2002, with a combination therapy with efavirenz. July 2022, for the first time since 2007, we noted a detectable HIV-1 VL of 134 copies/mL. After additional three months of DOR plus BIC/TAF/FTC, the HIV-1 VL rose to 187 copies/mL. Therapeutic adherence was strictly monitored, and it was found that the patient was not following the correct interval between divalent cation containing drugs and BIC. Close monitoring of the HIV-1 VL after three weeks showed a still elevated viral load of 217 copies/mL. A genotypic resistance test was carried out, which showed emergence of resistance to all the INSTIs, including BIC.","The paper describes a case report of a patient who experienced virological failure and the emergence of resistance mutations, indicating that sequences were obtained from such individuals."
40431710,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"Yes, the patient was part of a clinical trial for some periods of treatment.","In 2003, in the context of the TMC-125 trial, ART was switched to didanosine (ddI), abacavir (ABC), tenofovir disoproxil (TDF), lopinavir/ritonavir (LPV/r), and etravirine (ETR). This combination of drugs again failed to achieve viral suppression, as did the combination ddI/ABC/TDF and atazanavir/ritonavir (ATV/r) in 2005. Viral suppression first occurred in 2007 with the introduction of the integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) raltegravir (RAL) and the switch from ATV/r to DRV/r in combination with AZT/3TC and TDF. From then on, HIV-1 VL was fully suppressed below the limit of quantification of 50 copies/mL plasma, with CD4 + cell counts ranging from 690 cells/μL to 720 cells/μL. Treatment adherence was consistently reported to be optimal. A DNA-based genotypic resistance assay on proviral DNA in 2015, within the framework of the Clinical Trial CN518, revealed the additional emergence of the NRTI resistance mutation M184V. This led the treating team to discontinue AZT/3TC, while continuing TDF. At the same time, RAL was replaced by the meanwhile available dolutegravir (DTG) to strengthen the genetic resistance barrier. Two years later, in 2017, the therapy was switched to tenofovir alafenamide, emtricitabine, elvitegravir, in a single tablet regimen with the booster cobicistat (TAF/FTC/ELV/c) and DRV. Over the last ten years, the patient has manifested chronic primary headache and a depressive syndrome with insomnia, which eventually required a therapy with trazodone and zolpidem, a metabolic syndrome with grade 1 obesity, type 2 diabetes, and dyslipidemia.","The paper mentions that the patient was part of the TMC-125 trial and the Clinical Trial CN518, indicating that he was involved in clinical trials during his treatment."
40431710,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,Only one individual had samples obtained for HIV sequencing.,"We describe a rare case of virological failure on a quadruple therapy with BIC/3TC/TAF and DOR in a treatment-experienced patient. A DNA-based genotypic resistance assay on proviral DNA in 2015, within the framework of the Clinical Trial CN518, revealed the additional emergence of the NRTI resistance mutation M184V. Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank [1] taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL < 20 copies/mL) and on biobanked plasma (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier. This case report provides rare, well-documented insights into the development of bictegravir resistance and the rapid re-emergence of archived wild-type virus following interruption of antiretroviral therapy. The patient experienced multiple ARTs, close monitoring and inhibition of virus evolution by genotypic resistance tests, treatment interruption followed by ommission of RAMs, and resorption of ancient archived virus, which could not be done in a regular clinical cohort of patients. Hence, we report a single case. .","The paper focuses on a single case study of a 27-year-old male patient, the the concluding section describes it as a single case report, indicating that only one individual's samples were sequenced."
40431710,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.","A 27-year-old male was diagnosed with HIV-1 infection Subtype B in 1994. At that time, he was enrolled in the Swiss HIV Cohort Study. At diagnosis, HIV-1 viral load (VL) was 115,000 copies/mL, with a nadir CD4+ cell count of 200 cells/μL (9%). Zidovudine (AZT) and ritonavir (RTV) were initiated about a year later. At that time, the only HIV-related complication was oral leukoplakia. In 1996, due to never having reached a suppressed plasma viral load, a first genotypic resistance test on HIV-1 plasma RNA was performed and showed the mutation K70R associated with thymidine analog nucleoside (TAMs) reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTIs) resistance and a pattern of mutations (I54V, V82AT) associated with resistance to all protease inhibitors (PIs) and increased susceptibility to darunavir (DRV). During the first 8 years of treatment, antiretroviral therapy (ART) could only partially control HIV-1, with persistent viremia despite multiple different combinations of ART (Figure 1). Thus, in 2002 another genotypic resistance test was performed and the NRTI mutations K70R, M184V, T66I, Z67N, D67N, pol mutations, In  2003, the context of the TMC-125 trial, ART was switched to didanosine (ddI), abacavir (ABC), tenofovir disoproxil (TDF), lopinavir/ritonavir (LPV/r), and etravirine (ETR). This combination of drugs again failed to achieve viral suppression, as did the combination ddI/ABC/TDF and atazanavir/ritonavir (ATV/r) in 2005. Viral suppression first occurred in 2007 with the introduction of the integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) raltegravir (RAL) and the switch from ATV/r to DRV/r combination with AZT/3TC and TDF. From then on, HIV-1 VL was fully suppressed below the limit of quantification of 50 copies/mL plasma, with CD4 + cell counts ranging from 690 cells/μL to 720 cells/μL. Treatment adherence was consistently reported to be optimal. A DNA-based genotypic resistance assay proviral DNA in 2015, within the framework of the Clinical Trial CN518, revealed the additional emergence of the NRTI resistance mutation M184V. This led the treating team to discontinue AZT/3TC, while continuing TDF. At the same time, RAL was replaced by the meanwhile available doluteagrivir (DTG) to strengthen the genetic resistance barrier. Two years later, in 2017, the therapy was switched to tenofovir alafenamide, emtricitabine, elvitegravir, in a single tablet regimen with the booster cobicistat (TAF/FTC/ELV/c) and DRV. Over the last ten years, the patient has manifested chronic primary headache and a depressive syndrome with insomnia, which eventually required a therapy with trazodone and zolpidem, a metabolic syndrome with grade 1 obesity, type 2 diabetes, and dyslipidemia.","The paper describes a patient who had been receiving antiretroviral (ARV) drugs for many years, and the emergence of resistance to all four major ART classes, and the paper specifically focuses on the sequencing of HIV-1 from this patient, indicating that these sequences were obtained from individuals with prior ARV drug exposure."
40431710,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"The drug classes received by the individual before sample sequencing included NRTI, NNRTI, PI, and INSTI.","This case report provides rare, well-documented insights into the development of bictegravir resistance and the rapid re-emergence of archived wild-type virus following interruption of antiretroviral therapy. Its strengths include pharmacokinetic analysis, comprehensive resistance testing, exact drug and adherence history, next-generation sequencing from HIV-1 RNA and DNA and phylogenetic analysis. To identify potentially selected RAMs after initiation of the new drug regimen, a further genotypic resistance test was conducted two months after the start of the new antiretroviral therapy at HIV-1 RNA VL of 1590 copies/mL of plasma. This led the treating team to discontinue AZT/3TC, while continuing TDF. At the same time, RAL was replaced by the meanwhile available dolutegravir (DTG) to strengthen the genetic resistance barrier. Two years later, in 2017, the therapy was switched to tenofovir alafenamide, emtricitabine, elvitegravir, in a single tablet regimen with the booster cobicistat (TAF/FTC/ELV/c) and DRV. Over the last ten years, the patient has manifested chronic primary headache and a depressive syndrome with insomnia, which eventually required a therapy with trazodone and zolpidem, a metabolic syndrome with grade 1 obesity, type 2 diabetes, and dyslipidemia.","The paper mentions that the individual received drugs from all four major classes: NRTIs (e.g., zidovudine, tenofovir, abacavir), NNRTIs (e.g., efavirenz, rilpivirine, doravirine), PIs (e.g., ritonavir, lopinavir, atazanavir, darunavir), and INSTIs (e.g., raltegravir, dolutegravir, bictegravir), indicating that individuals in the study received drugs from all major drug classes before the samples for sequencing were obtained."
40431710,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"The drugs received by the individual before sample sequencing included Zidovudine (AZT), Didanosine (ddI), Abacavir (ABC), Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate(TDF), Lopinavir boosted with Ritonavir (LPV/r), Atazanavir boosted with Ritonavir (ATV/r), Etravirine (ETR), Darunavir boosted with Ritonavir (DRV/r), Raltegravir (RAL), Dolutegravir (DTG), and Bictegravir (BIC), Doravirine (DOR), Fostemsavir (FTR)","This case report provides rare, well-documented insights into the development of bictegravir resistance and the rapid re-emergence of archived wild-type virus following interruption of antiretroviral therapy. Its strengths include pharmacokinetic analysis, comprehensive resistance testing, exact drug and adherence history, next-generation sequencing from HIV-1 RNA and DNA and phylogenetic analysis. To identify potentially selected RAMs after initiation of the new drug regimen, a further genotypic resistance test was conducted two months after the start of the new antiretroviral therapy at HIV-1 RNA VL of 1590 copies/mL of plasma. This led the treating team to discontinue AZT/3TC, while continuing TDF. At the same time, RAL was replaced by the meanwhile available dolutegravir (DTG) to strengthen the genetic resistance barrier. Two years later, in 2017, the therapy was switched to tenofovir alafenamide, emtricitabine, elvitegravir, in a single tablet regimen with the booster cobicistat (TAF/FTC/ELV/c) and DRV. Over the last ten years, the patient has manifested chronic primary headache and a depressive syndrome with insomnia, which eventually required a therapy with trazodone and zolpidem, a metabolic syndrome with grade 1 obesity, type 2 diabetes, and dyslipidemia.","The paper provides a detailed list of the drugs that the individual received over the years, including their specific combinations, which were used prior to obtaining samples for sequencing."
40431710,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,"No, the sequences were not made publicly available due to patient privacy concerns.",The data that support the findings of this case report are not publicly available due to concerns regarding patient privacy and confidentiality. Data are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request and with appropriate institutional approvals.,"The paper states that the data supporting the findings are not publicly available, indicating that sequences were not made accessible."
40431742,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Samples from 1667 PLWHA were analyzed: 81.2% were male, with 52.6% identifying as men who have sex with men.","The study involved analyzing samples from 1667 people living with HIV/AIDS (PLWHA), indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
40431742,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Abstract: Background: Argentina has reported moderate to high levels of transmitted drug resistance in people living with HIV/AIDS (PLWHA), mostly to non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs). Doravirine (DOR) has a unique resistance profile and retains potent antiviral activity in the presence of the most prevalent NNRTI-associated resistant viruses. Scarce data exist regarding the frequency of DOR resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) in Latin America. We describe the prevalence of DOR RAMs in samples from adults PLWHA in Buenos Aires, Argentina, in the context of a survey of transmitted drug resistance (TDR). Material and Methods: A cross-sectional study was undertaken utilizing samples collected between 2017 and 2021 at two reference HIV clinics. Samples were analyzed for RAMs using the World Health Organization (WHO) mutation list. Mutations to DOR were assessed with the Stanford and Agence Nationale de Recherches sur le SIDA (ANRS) algorithms. Rilpivirine (RPV) RAMs were assessed using the Stanford algorithm. Susceptibility to NNRTIs was evaluated using the HIVdb Program with Stanford and ANRS criteria. Results: Samples from 1667 PLWHA were analyzed: 81.2% were male, with 52.6% identifying as men who have sex with men. According to the WHO list, the overall TDR was 12.1% (n = 203). The prevalence of RAMs was 10.1% (170/1667) for NNRTIs, 4% (67/1667) for nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs), and 1.7% (30/1667) for protease inhibitors (PIs). The most frequent NNRTI mutations were K103N (5.6%), G190A (0.89%), and K103S (0.77%). The prevalence of DOR RAMs was <2%, with the most common being Y188L (0.53%). Rilpivirine RAM prevalence was 6%. Susceptibility to DOR, RPV, efavirenz, and nevirapine as given by the Stanford algorithm was 97.4%, 92%, 91.4%, and 90.4%, respectively. The ANRS criteria yielded susceptibility rates of 98.3%, 93.3%, 92.3%, and 90.8%, respectively. Regarding NRTIs, thymidine analog mutations (including T215 revertants) were the most frequent RAMs. Among PIs, the most prevalent RAMs were M46L (0.47%) and V82A (0.35%). Conclusions: Our study shows the persistence of moderate to high levels of resistance to first-generation NNRTIs. Despite this, prevalence was low for DOR. Surveillance of TDR remains critical for recommendations of ART initiation.",The study does not mention in vitro drug susceptibility experiments or results; it primarily focuses on the prevalence of resistance mutations.
40431742,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
40431742,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,PR and RT,"HIV-1 resistance genotyping was used for the identification of RAMs in the HIV-1 polymerase (pol) gene. The process...a second nested PCR step for the HIV-1 pol fragment viral region codifying for Protease and Reverse transcriptase using the Applied Biosystem (Foster City, USA), AB Veriti 96-Well Thermal Cycler. The final PCR product was 1084 base pairs in length, encompassing the protease and reverse-transcriptase regions of the HIV-1 pol gene.","The study sequences the HIV-1 polymerase (pol) gene, which includes the Protease (PR) and Reverse Transcriptase (RT) regions."
40431742,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Argentina,"we describe the prevalence of dor ram's in samples from adults plwha in buenos aires, argentina, in the context of a survey of transmitted drug resistance (tdr).","The samples were obtained from two reference HIV clinics in Buenos Aires, Argentina."
40431742,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017-2021,Samples were collected between 2017 and 2021 at two reference HIV clinics.,The samples were collected over a period from 2017 to 2021.
40431742,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"The sequencing products were analyzed on an Applied Biosystems/Hitachi 3500 Genetic Analyzer (Waltham, MA, USA).","The paper explains the use of PCR amplification followed by sequencing on an Applied Biosystems/Hitachi 3500 Genetic Analyzer, indicating Sanger sequencing."
40431742,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40431742,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Samples from ART-naive PLWHA were retrospectively analyzed for RAMS associated with resistance to DOR and other drugs at a reference laboratory . All plasma samples were processed and stored for the determination of drug resistance mutations at the Laboratorio Dr. Stamboulian molecular biology division in Buenos Aires, Argentina, according to standardized procedures. Briefly, HIV-1 resistance genotyping was used for the identification of RAMs in the HIV-1 polymerase (pol) gene. The process comprises first the isolation and purification of plasma viral RNAs by MagNA Pure Compact (Roche, Basel, Switzerland), with the kit MagNA Pure Large Volume. Viral load measurement was performed using the Cobas® HIV-1 reagent, a nucleic acid test for in vitro diagnostic use on the ROCHE Cobas® 4800 System. The sample type used was EDTA-preserved plasma.",The study analyzed samples from plasma drawn as part of routine clinical practice.
40431742,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,The study was conducted using only structured secondary data. Samples from ART-naive PLWHA were retrospectively analyzed for RAMS associated with resistance to DOR and other drugs at a reference laboratory (Laboratorio Dr. Stamboulian).,"The inclusion criteria defined participants as treatment-naive, and the study focused on samples from ART-naive individuals."
40431742,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This study analyzed genotype results gathered from plasma samples drawn as part of routine clinical practice from naïve adult PLWHA from two reference HIV-clinics in Buenos Aires, Argentina, ensuring systematic testing of all collected samples. All plasma samples were processed and stored for the determination of drug resistance mutations at the Laboratorio Dr. Stamboulian molecular biology division in Buenos Aires, Argentina, according to standardized procedures.  The study was a retrospective cross-sectional, non-interventional study, conducted over the period 2017-09/2021.","The study was a retrospective cross-sectional, non-interventional study, not a clinical trial."
40431742,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1667,"For the mutation analysis, 1667 genotypes were included, as 10 did not have available results.",The study analyzed 1667 genotypes for the mutation analysis.
40431742,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"Inclusion Criteria: Treatment-naive, defined as no antiretrovirals (in combination or monotherapy) received before resistance test based on clinical records. Samples from ART-naive PLWHA were retrospectively analyzed for RAMS associated with resistance to DOR and other drugs at a reference laboratory . The study was conducted using only structured secondary data. Samples from ART-naive PLWHA were retrospectively analyzed for RAMS associated with resistance to DOR and other drugs at a reference laboratory (Laboratorio Dr. Stamboulian). All plasma samples were processed and stored for the determination of drug resistance mutations at the Laboratorio Dr. Stamboulian molecular biology division in Buenos Aires, Argentina, according to standardized procedures. 2.1. Study Design The study was a retrospective cross-sectional, non-interventional study, conducted over the period 2017-09/2021. The study was conducted using only structured secondary data. Samples from ART-naive PLWHA were retrospectively analyzed for RAMS associated with resistance to DOR and other drugs at a reference laboratory (Laboratorio Dr. Stamboulian). Graduate thesis of the study","The inclusion criteria defined participants as treatment-naive, and the study focused on samples from ART-naive individuals."
40431742,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"Inclusion Criteria: Treatment-naive, defined as no antiretrovirals (in combination or monotherapy) received before resistance test based on clinical records. Samples from ART-naive PLWHA were retrospectively analyzed for RAMS associated with resistance to DOR and other drugs at a reference laboratory . The study was conducted using only structured secondary data. Samples from ART-naive PLWHA were retrospectively analyzed for RAMS associated with resistance to DOR and other drugs at a reference laboratory (Laboratorio Dr. Stamboulian). All plasma samples were processed and stored for the determination of drug resistance mutations at the Laboratorio Dr. Stamboulian molecular biology division in Buenos Aires, Argentina, according to standardized procedures. 2.1. Study Design The study was a retrospective cross-sectional, non-interventional study, conducted over the period 2017-09/2021. The study was conducted using only structured secondary data. Samples from ART-naive PLWHA were retrospectively analyzed for RAMS associated with resistance to DOR and other drugs at a reference laboratory (Laboratorio Dr. Stamboulian). Graduate thesis of the study","The inclusion criteria defined participants as treatment-naive, and the study focused on samples from ART-naive individuals."
40431742,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"Inclusion Criteria: Treatment-naive, defined as no antiretrovirals (in combination or monotherapy) received before resistance test based on clinical records. Samples from ART-naive PLWHA were retrospectively analyzed for RAMS associated with resistance to DOR and other drugs at a reference laboratory . The study was conducted using only structured secondary data. Samples from ART-naive PLWHA were retrospectively analyzed for RAMS associated with resistance to DOR and other drugs at a reference laboratory (Laboratorio Dr. Stamboulian). All plasma samples were processed and stored for the determination of drug resistance mutations at the Laboratorio Dr. Stamboulian molecular biology division in Buenos Aires, Argentina, according to standardized procedures. 2.1. Study Design The study was a retrospective cross-sectional, non-interventional study, conducted over the period 2017-09/2021. The study was conducted using only structured secondary data. Samples from ART-naive PLWHA were retrospectively analyzed for RAMS associated with resistance to DOR and other drugs at a reference laboratory (Laboratorio Dr. Stamboulian). Graduate thesis of the study","The inclusion criteria defined participants as treatment-naive, and the study focused on samples from ART-naive individuals."
40431742,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any public availability of the sequences.
40490983,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Of 187 participants (median age 41 years, 54% female, 41% on atazanavir/ritonavir, 59% on lopinavir/ritonavir-based ART), 91% had any resistance (NRTI 79%, NNRTI 80%, major PI 37%, dual-class 36%, triple-class 37%).","The paper discusses the sequencing of HIV-1 RNA from plasma samples of participants, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
40490983,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on genotypic resistance and clinical outcomes rather than laboratory-based susceptibility testing.
40490983,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,,The paper does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
40490983,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Protease and reverse transcriptase,"Protease and reverse transcriptase resistance mutations were obtained from sequences requested from laboratories, and when unavailable, from genotyping reports from charts.",The paper mentions sequencing of the protease and reverse transcriptase regions to obtain resistance mutations.
40490983,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Kenya,"In this retrospective cohort study, we analysed medical records from PLWH at the Academic Model Providing Access to Healthcare (AMPATH) in western Kenya, which provides guidelines-based HIV services. In 2015, AMPATH established a Drug Resistance Clinic at Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital (MTRH) to provide multidisciplinary, patient-centred care for those with second-line failure.","The study was conducted at the Academic Model Providing Access to Healthcare (AMPATH) in western Kenya, and the samples were obtained from participants referred to the Drug Resistance Clinic."
40490983,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2011 to 2021,"We conducted a retrospective cohort study of people living with HIV (PLWH) ≥3 years old with second-line viral failure (VF, ≥1000 copies/ml) at the Academic Model Providing Access to Healthcare from 2011 to 2021. We assessed resistance prevalence and patterns at second-line VF, stratified by PI (atazanavir/ritonavir or lopinavir/ritonavir), and examined correlations of resistance and treatment strategies with VF at 6-18 months post-genotype. A total of 254 second-line failure genotypes were ordered at AMPATH from 2011 to 2021 (Figure 1). Of these, 60 were unretrievable or unverified and seven were done for children ≤3 years old. Thus, 187 PLWH with genotypes were analysed, with a median age at genotyping 41 years and 54% female (Table 1). The median genotyping year was 2017, earliest in 2011 (before Resistance Clinic started in 2015) and latest in 2021.",The paper states that the study included participants with samples obtained from 2011 to 2021.
40490983,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Sanger sequencing of plasma HIV-1 RNA was performed by the Kisumu Kenya Medical Research Institute and Nairobi National HIV Reference Laboratories [32, 33].",The paper mentions that Sanger sequencing was performed on plasma HIV-1 RNA.
40490983,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"All participants had no prior integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) exposure (including raltegravir) and integrase genotyping was not performed. Sequences passing quality control were analysed [34], with subtypes captured from available sequences using REGA. We treated all samples as bulk population sequences.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40490983,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma HIV-1 RNA,"Sanger sequencing of plasma HIV-1 RNA was performed by the Kisumu Kenya Medical Research Institute and Nairobi National HIV Reference Laboratories [32, 33].",The paper specifies that plasma HIV-1 RNA was sequenced.
40490983,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"We conducted a retrospective cohort study of people living with HIV (PLWH) ≥3 years old with second-line viral failure (VF, ≥1000 copies/ml) at the Academic Model Providing Access to Healthcare from 2011 to 2021. We assessed resistance prevalence and patterns at second-line VF, stratified by PI (atazanavir/ritonavir or lopinavir/ritonavir), and examined correlations of resistance and treatment strategies with VF at 6-18 months post-genotype. Most (171/187, 91%) participants had resistance at second-line failure: 79% NRTI (most common M184V-73%), 80% NNRTI (most common K103N-29%) and 37% major PI (most common M46I-27%) (Table 2 and Figure S2). Dual-(36%) or triple-(37%) class resistance was present in 73% of participants. NRTI and NNRTI mutation frequencies were similar among those on atazanavir/ritonavir versus lopinavir/ritonavir. However, more major PI mutations were detected upon atazanavir/ritonavir versus lopinavir/ritonavir VF (54% versus 26%) and certain mutations (150L/V and I84V) differed.","The paper focuses on participants with second-line viral failure, indicating that the sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure."
40490983,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We conducted a retrospective cohort study of people living with HIV (PLWH) ≥3 years old with second-line viral failure (VF, ≥1000 copies/ml) at the Academic Model Providing Access to Healthcare from 2011 to 2021.","The paper describes a retrospective cohort study, not a clinical trial."
40490983,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,187,"A total of 254 second-line failure genotypes were ordered at AMPATH from 2011 to 2021 (Figure 1). Of these, 60 were unretrievable or unverified and seven were done for children ≤3 years old. Thus, 187 PLWH with genotypes were analysed, with a median age at genotyping 41 years and 54% female (Table 1).",The paper states that 187 participants with genotypes were included in the analysis.
40490983,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"The median ART duration at genotyping was 9 years, including 4 years on first- and 4 years on second-line. At genotyping, 76/187 were on atazanavir/ritonavir (42% had prior lopinavir/ritonavir exposure) and 111 on lopinavir/ritonavir (none with atazanavir/ritonavir exposure).","The paper mentions that participants had been on various ART regimens, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
40490983,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI","During the study, recommended anchor drugs for first-line regimens included efavirenz or nevirapine for individuals  ≥ 3 years; second-line regimens included atazanavir/ritonavir or lopinavir/ritonavir plus two NRTIs [16, 18, 30].",The paper lists the drug classes used in the regimens of the individuals whose samples were sequenced.
40490983,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Emtricitabine, Zidovudine, Lamuvidine, Efavirenz, Nevirapine, Atazanavir, Ritonavir, Lopinavir Abacavir","During the study, recommended anchor drugs for first-line regimens included efavirenz or nevirapine for individuals  ≥ 3 years; second-line regimens included atazanavir/ritonavir or lopinavir/ritonavir plus two NRTIs [16, 18, 30]. Regimens were guidelines-unsupported if participants remained on their second-line PI or switched to an alternative second-line PI, despite low- or higher-level resistance to either PI. Those with ≥2 class resistance who switched to third-line despite predicted resistance to their third-line PI (darunavir/ritonavir) or NNRTI (etravirine) drug were also considered guidelines-supported if regimens contained dolutegravir or raltegravir (assumed susceptibility given no prior INSTI exposure). Kenya guidelines. Summary of evolving Kenya guidelines for possible 3rd-line ART in children, adolescents and adults during the study period, 2011-2021: after defined VL after 2nd line succuess switch to DRV/r/3TC/TDF (2018-2021).",The paper provides details on the specific drugs that were part of the participants' regimens prior to sequencing.
40490983,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention that the sequences were made publicly available.
40573423,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,The study included 487 PWH...the genotypes of 487 PWH diagnosed from 2021 to 2024 were analyzed.,"The study included 487 PWH: 99 from 2021, 143 from 2022, 148 from 2023, and 97 from 2024. The genotypes of 487 PWH diagnosed from 2021 to 2024 were analyzed.","The paper mentions that it included 487 PWH and analyzed their genotypes, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
40573423,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"In this study, we investigated the proportion of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) mutations...to inform more effective treatment strategies.","In this study, we investigated the proportion of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) mutations and human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)-1 subtypes among 487 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve individuals in South Korea from 2021 to 2024 to inform more effective treatment strategies. Consistent with previous reports, subtype B was most prevalent among HIV-1 subtypes at 50.7% ; however, its proportion decreased annually (p = 0.047) . Various subtypes of circulating recombinant forms (CRFs) were analyzed in this study, resulting in high genetic diversity. The subtype distributions of Korean and non-Korean patients differed, with subtype B (53.7%) and CRF01_AE (34.4%) being dominant in the former and latter, respectively. TDR across antiretroviral drug classes was approximately 3.5% in South Korea. Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors elicited the greatest drug resistance, which increased from 2021 to 2023, with a slight decrease in  2024. The integrase strand transfer inhibitor drugs, elvitegravir and raltegravir, most frequently exhibited high resistance scores. We provide a comprehensive overview of the HIV-1 genetic distribution and TDR patterns in South Korea from 2021 to 2024. Within the broader context of HIV-1 epidemiology in Asia and the Pacific, the findings contribute to a comprehensive understanding of the global distribution of HIV-1 resistance and genotypes, enabling the development of effective interventions.","The paper discusses the analysis of transmitted drug resistance mutations and the prevalence of drug resistance across various antiretroviral drug classes, which implies that the study involved evaluating the drug resistance mutations associated with treatment-naïve individuals."
40573423,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No GenBank accession numbers are provided for the sequenced HIV isolates.,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that they were not provided."
40573423,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Envelope (env), gag, pol, protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes were sequenced.","Reverse transcription and nested PCR were performed to amplify the envelope (env), gag protein (gag), and pol protein (pol) for genotyping and the protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes for drug resistance analysis.","The paper lists multiple genes, including env, gag, pol, protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase, that were amplified and sequenced for both genotyping and drug resistance analysis."
40573423,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"The sequenced samples were obtained from South Korea, primarily from Korean and non-Korean individuals.","In South Korea, HIV confirmatory testing is conducted by the Korea Disease Control and Prevention Agency (KDCA) and 17 provincial Institutes of Health and Environment. All positive specimens nationwide are sent to the KDCA for surveillance to monitor recent HIV infection [15]. Demographic data were obtained from the Division of HIV/AIDS Prevention and Control at the KDCA. Recently diagnosed PWH who had not been exposed to ART were included.",The paper specifies that the study was conducted in South Korea and included HIV-1 isolates from 487 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve individuals in the country.
40573423,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained from 2021 to 2024.,"The study included 487 PWH: 99 from 2021, 143 from 2022, 148 from 2023, and 97 from 2024.",The paper indicates that the samples were collected over a range of years from 2021 to 2024.
40573423,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing was used.,Sanger sequencing was performed using in-house sequencing primers (Table 1).,The paper explicitly states that Sanger sequencing was the method used for sequencing the HIV samples.
40573423,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,"No, samples were not cloned prior to sequencing.","Reverse transcription and nested PCR were performed to amplify the envelope (env), gag protein (gag), and pol protein (pol) for genotyping and the protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes for drug resistance analysis.","The paper describes the reverse transcription and nested PCR amplification followed by sequencing, indicating that samples were not cloned prior to sequencing."
40573423,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma or serum samples were sequenced.,"Viral RNA was extracted from 140 μL of plasma or serum using the QIAcube extraction system (QIAGEN, Hilden, Germany), according to the manufacturer's instructions.",The paper mentions that viral RNA was extracted from plasma or serum for sequencing.
40573423,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"No, the study did not include individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen.","In this study, we investigated the proportion of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) mutations and human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)-1 subtypes among 487 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve individuals in South Korea from 2021 to 2024 to inform more effective treatment strategies.","The paper focused on 487 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve individuals, meaning they had not been treated and were not experiencing virological failure."
40573423,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"No, the paper does not mention that the patients were in a clinical trial.",All the authors hereby declare that all the experiments were reviewed and granted an exemption from ethical approval by the appropriate ethics committee (the KDCA Institutional Review Board Ethics Committee; approval number: KDCA-2025-01-03-PE-01). The requirement for informed consent from study participants was also waived by the KDCA Institutional Review Board. Demographic data were obtained from the Division of HIV/AIDS Prevention and Control at the KDCA. Recently diagnosed PWH who had not been exposed to ART were included. The initial diagnosis for all included PWH was made between January 2021 and October 2024.,The paper does not indicate that the patients were part of a clinical trial; they were included based on specific epidemiological and treatment-naïve criteria.
40573423,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,Samples for HIV sequencing were obtained from 487 individuals.,"The study included 487 PWH: 99 from 2021, 143 from 2022, 148 from 2023, and 97 from 2024.","The paper states that 487 PWH were included in the study, and their samples were used for HIV sequencing."
40573423,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"No, the paper does not report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.",Recently diagnosed PWH who had not been exposed to ART were included. The initial diagnosis for all included PWH was made between January 2021 and October 2024.,"The paper focused on recently diagnosed PWH who had not been exposed to ART, meaning they had not received ARV drugs before the study."
40573423,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No drug classes were received by individuals before sample sequencing; they were treatment-naïve.,The study investigated the proportion of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) mutations and human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)-1 subtypes among 487 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve individuals. Recently diagnosed PWH who had not been exposed to ART were included. All the authors hereby declare that all the experiments were reviewed and granted an exemption from ethical approval by the appropriate ethics committee (the KDCA Institutional Review Board Ethics Committee; approval number: KDCA-2025-01-03-PE-01). The requirement for informed consent from study participants was also waived by the KDCA Institutional Review Board.,"The paper specifically included ART-naïve individuals, meaning they had not received any antiretroviral drugs before the study."
40573423,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing; they were treatment-naïve.,"In this study, we investigated the proportion of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) mutations and human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)-1 subtypes among 487 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve individuals in South Korea from 2021 to 2024 to inform more effective treatment strategies. All the authors hereby declare that all the experiments were reviewed and granted an exemption from ethical approval by the appropriate ethics committee (the KDCA Institutional Review Board Ethics Committee; approval number: KDCA-2025-01-03-PE-01). The requirement for informed consent from study participants was also waived by the KDCA Institutional Review Board. Recently diagnosed PWH who had not been exposed to ART were included. The initial diagnosis for all included PWH was made between January 2021 and October 2024.","The paper included 487 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve individuals, meaning they had not received any antiretroviral drugs prior to sample sequencing."
40573423,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,The data used to support this study are available from the corresponding author upon request.,Data Availability Statement: The data used to support this study are available from the corresponding author upon request.,"The paper includes a data availability statement indicating that the data can be requested from the corresponding author, suggesting that the sequences were made publicly available."
40596906,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"Genotypic sequences of the HIV pol region (protease/reverse transcriptase-PR/RT) were obtained using the ViroSeq Genotyping System, carried out in the laboratories of the National Genotyping Network (RENAGENO) coordinated by the Department of HIV/AIDS of the Brazilian Ministry of Health (GenBank accession number PV526713-PV526742).","Genotypic sequences of the HIV pol region (protease/reverse transcriptase-PR/RT) were obtained using the ViroSeq Genotyping System, carried out in the laboratories of the National Genotyping Network (RENAGENO) coordinated by the Department of HIV/AIDS of the Brazilian Ministry of Health.","The paper describes the analysis of HIV-1 sequences from mother-child pairs, indicating that HIV sequences were reported from patient samples."
40596906,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No in vitro drug susceptibility data reported,"Methods This study included 15 mother-child pairs with confirmed vertical transmission of HIV-1. The genotyping sequences were provided by the Brazilian Ministry of Health. The analyses included descriptions of antiretroviral resistance mutation profiles of the mothers and children, subtype determination, and phylogenetic analyses.",The methods section mentions sequencing of patient samples but does not mention any phenotpic or genotypic susceptibility test performed on drugs
40596906,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,PV526713-PV526742,"Genotypic sequences of the HIV pol region (protease/reverse transcriptase-PR/RT) were obtained using the ViroSeq Genotyping System, carried out in the laboratories of the National Genotyping Network (RENAGENO) coordinated by the Department of HIV/AIDS of the Brazilian Ministry of Health (GenBank accession number PV526713-PV526742).","The paper provides specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, which are PV526713 to PV526742."
40596906,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,HIV pol region (protease/reverse transcriptase-PR/RT),"Genotypic sequences of the HIV pol region (protease/reverse transcriptase-PR/RT) were obtained using the ViroSeq Genotyping System, carried out in the laboratories of the National Genotyping Network (RENAGENO) coordinated by the Department of HIV/AIDS of the Brazilian Ministry of Health (GenBank accession number PV526713-PV526742).","The paper specifies that the genotypic sequences of the HIV pol region, which includes protease and reverse transcriptase, were obtained and analyzed."
40596906,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Brazil,"This study aimed to describe the transmission of drug-resistant HIV-1 variants and phylogenetic characterisation of viral strains in mother-child pairs from the cities of Recife and São Luís, located in the Northeast region of Brazil, in 2007–2022.","The study was conducted in Brazil, specifically in the cities of Recife and São Luís, and the samples were obtained from these locations."
40596906,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2007–2022,"This study aimed to describe the transmission of drug-resistant HIV-1 variants and phylogenetic characterisation of viral strains in mother-child pairs from the cities of Recife and São Luís, located in the Northeast region of Brazil, in 2007–2022.","The study period mentioned in the abstract is 2007–2022, indicating the range of years from which the sequenced samples were obtained."
40596906,7,What method was used for sequencing?,ViroSeq Genotyping System,"Genotypic sequences of the HIV pol region (protease/reverse transcriptase-PR/RT) were obtained using the ViroSeq Genotyping System, carried out in the laboratories of the National Genotyping Network (RENAGENO) coordinated by the Department of HIV/AIDS of the Brazilian Ministry of Health (GenBank accession number PV526713-PV526742).",The paper states that the genotypic sequences were obtained using the ViroSeq Genotyping System.
40596906,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence.,"The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, indicating that this step was not performed."
40596906,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Clinical samples from patients diagnosed with HIV infection,"Among the 15 mother-child pairs, eight (53%) were from Maranhão and seven (47%) from Pernambuco. Of the children, eight (53%) were male, and 10 (66.7%) started ART within the first 2 years of life. In one case (6.7%), no record of ART initiation was found. Nine children (60%) had initiated ART, including zidovudine (ZDV) + lamivudine (3TC) + nevirapine (NVP) in three (20%), ZV + 3TC + lopinavir/ritonavir in two (13.3%), and ZV + 3TC + efavirenz (EFV) in two (6.7%), while no treatment was described for three (20%)","The paper mentions the samples were from mothers and children in Brazil who were in therapeuti  follow up, which indicates the samples were legally obtained and case reports were collected after the treatment started, which means the samples were from clinical specimens."
40596906,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,The mothers included both treatment-naive women diagnosed during pregnancy who underwent pretreatment genotyping and those living with HIV-1 during prior treatment who required genotyping due to virological failure.,"The paper states that some mothers living with HIV-1 during prior treatment required genotyping due to virological failure, indicating sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure."
40596906,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This study was conducted from October 2021 to September 2023 in São Luís, Maranhão, and Recife, Pernambuco, at the High-Risk Maternity Hospital in Imperatriz, Pediatric Infectious Disease Outpatient Clinic at the University Hospital of the Federal University of Maranhão, and Pediatric Infectious Disease Outpatient Clinic at the University Hospital Oswaldo Cruz of the University of Pernambuco. The study included 15 mother-child pairs diagnosed with HIV-1 infections who were under therapeutic follow-up at the selected reference units.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial; it describes a study conducted at specific hospitals and outpatient clinics.
40596906,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,"15 mother-child pairs, total 30 individuals","The study included 15 mother-child pairs diagnosed with HIV-1 infections who were under therapeutic follow-up at the selected reference units. Among the 15 mother-child pairs, eight (53%) were from Maranhão and seven (47%) from Pernambuco.","The paper states that the study included 15 mother-child pairs, totaling 30 individuals from whom samples were sequenced."
40596906,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,The mothers included both treatment-naive women diagnosed during pregnancy who underwent pretreatment genotyping and those living with HIV-1 during prior treatment who required genotyping due to virological failure.,"The paper mentions that some mothers living with HIV-1 during prior treatment required genotyping, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
40596906,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,NRTIs and NNRTIs,"Among the NRTI resistance-associated mutations, M184V was the most frequent (26.7%) (Fig. 2). The K219KR and K70E mutations were observed in only one mother (6.7%) (Fig. 2). The M184I, M41L, and T215FY mutations were identified in only one child each (6.7%) . Regarding NNRTI resistance-associated mutations, K103N was identified in four patients (26.7%) in both groups. The G190A mutation was more frequent in the child group (n = 3 patients [20%]) than in the mother group (n = 2 [13.3%]) (Fig. 2).","The paper discusses resistance mutations associated with nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) and non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs), indicating that individuals had received these drug classes before sample sequencing."
40596906,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"zidovudine (ZDV), lamivudine (3TC), nevirapine (NVP), lopinavir, ritonavir, and efavirenz (EFV)","Nine children (60%) had initiated ART, including zidovudine (ZDV) + lamivudine (3TC) + nevirapine (NVP) in three (20%), ZV + 3TC + lopinavir/ritonavir in two (13.3%), and ZV + 3TC + efavirenz (EFV) in two (6.7%), while no treatment was described for three (20%)","The paper lists the specific drugs that were part of the ART regimens initiated by the children, including zidovudine (ZDV), lamivudine (3TC), nevirapine (NVP), lopinavir, ritonavir, and efavirenz (EFV)."
40596906,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"Genotypic sequences of the HIV pol region (protease/reverse transcriptase-PR/RT) were obtained using the ViroSeq Genotyping System, carried out in the laboratories of the National Genotyping Network (RENAGENO) coordinated by the Department of HIV/AIDS of the Brazilian Ministry of Health (GenBank accession number PV526713-PV526742).","The paper provides the GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that the sequences were made publicly available."
