40779404,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,Post hoc analysis evaluating virologic outcomes in VS individuals with preexisting K65R/N mutations documented by historical genotypes and/or baseline proviral DNA sequencing who received B/F/TAF in 6 Phase 2/3/3b clinical trials.,"The paper mentions the collection and analysis of historical genotypic data from patient samples, indicating that HIV sequences were reported from patient samples."
40779404,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Efficacy of Bictegravir/Emtricitabine/Tenofovir Alafenamide in Participants With Preexisting K65R/N in HIV-1 in Phase 2/3/3b Clinical Trials

Background: Preexisting HIV-1 drug resistance affects antiretroviral treatment options for people with HIV-1 (PWH). Prior studies have demonstrated safety and efficacy of bictegravir/emtricitabine/tenofovir (TFV) alafenamide [B/F/tenofovir alafenamide fumarate (TAF)] in PWH including in virologically suppressed (VS) individuals with M184V/I mutations in reverse transcriptase that confer resistance to emtricitabine. K65R/N mutations in reverse transcriptase are selected by and confer 2-3-fold reduced susceptibility to TFV or its prodrugs TFV disoproxil fumarate (TDF) and TFV alafenamide fumarate (TAF) in vitro.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility assays or data, focusing instead on clinical outcomes and genotypic analyses."
40779404,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, focusing on clinical trial data and resistance mutations instead.","GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates are typically provided in the methods or results sections of a paper, and their absence suggests that they were not reported."
40779404,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT, IN","Historical RNA or proviral DNA genotyping reports for protease (PR), RT, and integrase (IN), from local laboratories or commercial sites were collected if available. Retrospective proviral DNA genotyping of PR/RT/IN using GenoSure Archive (Monogram Biosciences, CA), which uses NGS technology was performed on BL whole blood samples. Bioinformatic filters removed APOBEC-mediated, hypermutated deep-sequence reads from GenoSure Archive results to prevent RAM overreporting (E138K, M184I, and M230I in RT, and G163R in IN). Genotyping data were reported on a population sequencing level.","The paper reports on the sequencing of the protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN) genes, as mentioned in the methods section."
40779404,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,No evidence,No evidence,"The paper does not specify the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained, focusing instead on the clinical trials."
40779404,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,No evidence,No evidence,The paper does not provide specific years for when the sequenced samples were obtained.
40779404,7,What method was used for sequencing?,NGS,"Retrospective proviral DNA genotyping of PR/RT/IN using GenoSure Archive (Monogram Biosciences, CA), which uses NGS technology was performed on BL whole blood samples.","The paper mentions that the GenoSure Archive uses NGS technology for genotyping, indicating that next-generation sequencing was used."
40779404,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Retrospective proviral DNA genotyping of PR/RT/IN using GenoSure Archive (Monogram Biosciences, CA), which uses NGS technology was performed on BL whole blood samples. Bioinformatic filters removed APOBEC-mediated, hypermutated deep-sequence reads from GenoSure Archive results to prevent RAM overreporting (E138K, M184I, and M230I in RT, and G163R in IN).","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, indicating that direct sequencing from clinical samples was performed."
40779404,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Whole blood samples,"Retrospective proviral DNA genotyping of PR/RT/IN using GenoSure Archive (Monogram Biosciences, CA), which uses NGS technology was performed on BL whole blood samples. The trial designs were as follows: Studies 1844 and 4030 were double-blinded placebo controlled, Studies 1878 and 4580 were randomized open-label, and Studies 1474 and 4449 were single-arm open label. In Studies 1844, 1878, 4580, and 4030, participants receiving a comparator regimen switched to B/F/TAF at week 24 (4580) or in an open-label extension (OLE) phase after week 48 (1844, 1878, and 4030). Participants with documented","The paper mentions that genotyping was performed on baseline whole blood samples, indicating that whole blood was the clinical specimen sequenced."
40779404,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"All participants with preexisting K65R/N receiving B/F/TAF (n = 20/20) had virologic suppression (HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL) through end of study, with no virologic failure or treatment-emergent resistance to B/F/TAF. Preexisting K65R/N mutations were detected in 20 of 2028 VS participants who received B/F/TAF. Frequency of K65R/N in the 6 studies ranged between 0% and 1.5%, with most participants (95%; n = 19/20) having K65R. Overall, K65R/N mutations were most commonly observed with other resistance-associated mutations (70.0%, n = 14/20), including M184V/I (45.0%, n = 9/20), and ≥1 thymidine analog mutations (30.0%, n = 6/20).","The participants had maintained virologic suppression on their regimen, and the study focuses on the efficacy of B/F/TAF in those with preexisting mutations."
40779404,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"From the Phase 2/3/3b B/F/TAF Studies GS-US-380-1474 (NCT02881320),[12] GS-US-380-1844 (NCT02603120),[15] GS-US-380-1878 (NCT02603107),[11] GS-US-380-4030 (NCT03110380),[16] and GS-US-380-4580 (NCT03631732), VS adults and children (>2 and < 18 years old) who received B/F/TAF were identified, and participants with baseline (BL) RT genotypic data were included in the analysis. Historical RNA or proviral DNA genotyping reports for protease (PR), RT, and integrase (IN), from local laboratories or commercial sites were collected if available.",The study involves post hoc analysis of data from multiple Phase 2/3/3b clinical trials.
40779404,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,2028,"From the 2182 participants who received B/F/TAF, 2028 participants had BL genotypic RT data.","The paper states that 2028 participants had baseline genotypic RT data, indicating the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing."
40779404,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Before enrollment, participants had been treated with TFV (80.0%), INSTIs (50.0%), non-nucleoside RT inhibitors (NNRTI; 45.0%), and PR inhibitors (PI; 75.0%) (Table 1). Detailed ART histories of participants with preexisting K65R/N are listed in Table 1, Supplemental Digital Content, http://links.lww.com/QAI/C557. K65R/N was detected in 20 of 2028 (1.0%) of participants. K65E was not detected in any of these participants.","The paper mentions that participants had been treated with various ARV drugs before enrollment, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals with prior ARV experience."
40779404,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","Before enrollment, participants had been treated with TFV (80.0%), INSTIs (50.0%), non-nucleoside RT inhibitors (NNRTI; 45.0%), and PR inhibitors (PI; 75.0%) (Table 1). Detailed ART histories of participants with preexisting K65R/N are listed in Table 1, Supplemental Digital Content, http://links.lww.com/QAI/C557. K65R/N was detected in 20 of 2028 (1.0%) of participants. K65E was not detected in any of these participants.","The paper lists the classes of drugs participants had been treated with before enrollment, including NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs."
40779404,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir (TFV) Alafenamide (TAF),","Before enrollment, participants had been treated with TFV (80.0%), INSTIs (50.0%), non-nucleoside RT inhibitors (NNRTI; 45.0%), and PR inhibitors (PI; 75.0%) (Table 1). Detailed ART histories of participants with preexisting K65R/N are listed in Table 1, Supplemental Digital Content, http://links.lww.com/QAI/C557. K65R/N was detected in 20 of 2028 (1.0%) of participants. K65E was not detected in any of these participants.",The paper does not provide specific drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing.
40779404,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, focusing on clinical trial data and resistance mutations instead.","The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, focusing on clinical trial data and resistance mutations instead."
40839365,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase.","The paper reports that samples from participants with confirmed virologic failure were genotyped, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples were obtained."
40839365,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No specific evidence,The paper discusses observed drug resistance mutations but does not mention any in vitro testing or susceptiblity data results.
40839365,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,No specific evidence,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, as they are typically included in the methods or results sections, which are not provided here."
40839365,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase","Prevalence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) to nucleotide reverse-transcriptase inhibitors was 22.6% (95% CI: 17.4% to 27.8%), to non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors 55.1% (95% CI 48.2% to 62.1%), and to protease inhibitors 4.9% (95% CI 1.3% to 8.5%).","The paper states the genes were genotyped with high rates of amplification, and the drug resistance testing was conducted on the required genes."
40839365,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Malawi,A cross-sectional design was used to assess drug resistance among adult PLHIV with virologic nonsuppression in Malawi between July 2022 and February 2023.,"The study was conducted in Malawi, and the participants were enrolled from various ART clinics within the country."
40839365,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022-2023,The survey was conducted between July 2022 and February 2023.,"The paper mentions the survey was conducted between 2022-2023, and the samples were collected during this period."
40839365,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,gene fragments of protease and reverse transcriptase and integrase were amplified and sequenced using HIV-1 Genotyping kit with Integrase and the 3500XL Genetic Analyzers (Applied Biosystems). Sanger sequence electropherograms were analyzed by using RECall (v2.32)—Web-based sequence analysis software.,"The paper mentions the sequencing method used for genotyping the HIV isolates, which is Sanger sequencing."
40839365,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No specific evidence,"The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, confirming they were not cloned."
40839365,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,Enrolled PLHIV submitted plasma samples for VL testing and those with VL ≥ 1000 copies/mL (confirmed virologic failure) underwent genotypic HIVDR testing.,The paper specifies that plasma samples were used for VL testing and subsequently for genotypic HIVDR testing.
40839365,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase.","The paper reports that samples were obtained from participants with confirmed virologic failure, indicating that these individuals were not achieving viral suppression on their treatment regimen."
40839365,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"A cross-sectional design was used to assess drug resistance among adult PLHIV with virologic nonsuppression in Malawi between July 2022 and February 2023. Eligible participants were PLHIV aged 15 years or older, on a DBR for at least 9 months, latest VL was ␀>000>000>000655ccopies/mL, and had completed at least 1 session of IAC per national guidelines.","The study is a cross-sectional survey, not a clinical trial."
40839365,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,212,"Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped.","The paper states that out of 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped."
40839365,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Individuals who resuppressed had been on DBR for a longer duration (2.7 vs. 2.4 years, P = 0.0011) and more often had an initial lower VL than those who had confirmed virologic failure. No significant differences were observed between the 2 groups in mean body mass index at the start of ART, mean overall duration on ART, and having no ART exposure before current DBR.","The paper mentions the duration of being on the current regimen, and also the had not been on treatment before the dolutegravir-based regimen, meaning they were treatment experienced."
40839365,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, Integrase Inhibitor","Individuals who resuppressed had been on DBR for a longer duration (2.7 vs. 2.4 years, P = 0.0011) and more often had an initial lower VL than those who had confirmed virologic failure. No significant differences were observed between the 2 groups in mean body mass index at the start of ART, mean overall duration on ART, and having no ART exposure before current DBR. The cohort also included participants receiving the same non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitor efavirenz generics, and integrase inhibitors, and all received tenofovir disoproxil fumarate and lamivudine, both n = 1; 24.9% integrase strand transfer inhibitors","The paper mentions the class of drugs that they had been on, which all had been sequenced in the study."
40839365,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Abacavir, emtricitabine, lamivudine, atazanavir, darunavir, efavirenz, zidovudine, tenofovir DF, raltegravir, dolutegravir","Individuals who resuppressed had been on DBR for a longer duration (2.7 vs. 2.4 years, P = 0.0011) and more often had an initial lower VL than those who had confirmed virologic failure. No significant differences were observed between the 2 groups in mean body mass index at the start of ART, mean overall duration on ART, and having no ART exposure before current DBR. The cohort also included participants receiving the same non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitor efavirenz generics, and integrase inhibitors, and all received tenofovir disoproxil fumarate and lamivudine, both n = 1; 24.9% integrase strand transfer inhibitors","The paper details the specific drugs that participants were receiving before sample sequencing, all had been seqenced in the isolates."
40839365,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No specific evidence,"The paper does not mention that the sequences were made publicly available, indicating they were not."
40857111,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"This subanalysis reports HIV-1 resistance results to the end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF, n = 122) and to study end (B/F/TAF, n = 119; DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF, n = 89). Baseline resistance was determined using historical and screening genotypes of protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN), if available. Participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing of PR/RT/IN.","The paper discusses the analysis of HIV-1 resistance results and mentions the determination of baseline resistance using historical and screening genotypes from patient samples, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
40857111,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods: After the 96-week randomized phase of ALLIANCE, participants could enter a 48-week open-label B/F/TAF-only phase. This subanalysis reports HIV-1 resistance results to the end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF, n = 122) and to study end (B/F/TAF, n = 119; DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF, n = 89). Baseline resistance was determined using historical and screening genotypes of protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN), if available. Participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing of PR/RT/IN; those subsequently not achieving virologic suppression (VS; HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL) were included in final resistance analysis population (RAP).",The paper focuses on clinical trial data and resistance testing from patient samples rather than reporting in vitro drug susceptibility data.
40857111,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,,"The paper does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, which are typically included in the methods or results sections as a standard practice for studies involving genetic sequences. However, these were not explicitly mentioned in the provided content."
40857111,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease (PR), Reverse Transcriptase (RT), Integrase (IN)","Baseline resistance was determined using historical and screening genotypes of protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN), if available. Participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing of PR/RT/IN; those subsequently not achieving virologic suppression (VS; HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL) were included in final resistance analysis population (RAP).","The paper explicitly mentions that baseline resistance was determined using genotypes of protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase."
40857111,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,,,The paper does not specify the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained.
40857111,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,,,The paper does not provide specific years during which the sequenced samples were obtained.
40857111,7,What method was used for sequencing?,,,The paper does not specify the exact sequencing method used for obtaining the HIV sequences.
40857111,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Assessments of HIV-1 viral load, HIV-1 subtype, and of baseline and postbaseline HIV-1 resistance were performed as described previously.[15] RAMs in protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN) are based on the International Antiviral Society-USA list[9] and are detailed in our previous publication.[15]",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40857111,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"In brief, participants were aged ≥18 years, with HIV-1 RNA ≥500 copies/mL and HBV DNA ≥2000 IU/mL. All participants provided written informed consent, and the study was undertaken in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki and following site-specific ethical approval. During the randomized phase, participants continued masked treatment until the final participant had completed their Week 96 assessment. Participants were then requested to attend an end-of-masked-treatment visit. At this visit, participants from both treatment groups could enter the OLE phase and receive B/F/TAF for a further 48 weeks. Efficacy findings for the 48-week OLE phase will be reported separately. Assessments of HIV-1 viral load, HIV-1 subtype, and of baseline and postbaseline HIV-1 resistance were performed as described previously.[15] Virologic outcomes were defined by the last on-treatment observation carried forward (LOCF) method. Virologic failure (VF) was defined as any of the following: confirmed virologic rebound (HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL after achieving <50 copies/mL, subsequently confirmed at the following visit; >1 log10 increase from nadir, subsequently confirmed at the following visit) or HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL at last visit while receiving study drug. Participants meeting these criteria, and with HIV-1 RNA ≥200 copies/mL at the confirmation visit or last visit on study drugs, underwent genotypic/phenotypic resistance analyses for PR/RT/IN. The final RAP for the masked phase and at study end included all participants who experienced VF (HIV-1 RNA ≥200 copies/mL) and did not subsequently achieve resuppression (HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL) on study drugs. There were 3 final RAP groups based on study treatment in the different phases of the study: (1) participants who received DTG+F/TDF in the randomized phase, analyzed through end of masked treatment; (2) participants who received B/F/TAF in the randomized phase, irrespective of receiving B/F/TAF in the OLE phase, analyzed through end of study; and (3) participants who received DTG+F/TDF in the randomized phase and then received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in the OLE phase, analyzed from study start through end of study. Fisher exact test was used to compare the proportions of participants with HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL at end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF recipients) or at study end (B/F/TAF recipients), by virologic categories, and the proportions of participants meeting the criteria for inclusion in the final RAP groups","The paper mentions that participants had HIV-1 RNA at ≥500 copies/mL and HBV DNA at ≥2000 IU/mL., which suggests that plasma viral load was measured and used for resistance testing."
40857111,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing of PR/RT/IN; those subsequently not achieving virologic suppression (VS; HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL) were included in final resistance analysis population (RAP). The final RAP for the masked phase and at study end included all participants who experienced VF (HIV-1 RNA ≥200 copies/mL) and did not subsequently achieve resuppression (HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL) on study drugs.  virologic failure (VF) was defined as any of the following: confirmed virologic rebound (HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL after achieving <50 copies/mL, subsequently confirmed at the following visit; >1 log10 increase from nadir, subsequently confirmed at the following visit) or HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL at last visit while receiving study drug. Participants meeting these criteria, and with HIV-1 RNA ≥200 copies/mL at the confirmation visit or last visit on study drugs, underwent genotypic/phenotypic resistance analyses for PR/RT/IN. The design of the phase 3 randomized ALLIANCE study (GS-US-380-4458; NCT03547908) has been described previously.[14] In brief, participants were aged ≥18 years, with HIV-1 RNA ≥500 copies/mL and HBV DNA ≥2000 IU/mL. All participants provided written informed consent, and the study was undertaken in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki and following site-specific ethical approval. During the randomized phase, participants continued masked treatment until the final participant had completed their Week 96 assessment. Participants were then requested to attend an end-of-masked-treatment visit. At this visit, participants from both treatment groups could enter the OLE phase and receive B/F/TAF for a further 48 weeks. Efficacy findings for the 48-week OLE phase will be reported separately. Assessments of HIV-1 viral load, HIV-1 subtype, and of baseline and postbaseline HIV-1 resistance were performed as described previously.[15]","The paper mentions that participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing, confirming that sequences were obtained from such individuals."
40857111,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"Background: ALLIANCE is a phase 3 randomized study of bibtegravir/emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide (B/F/TAF) versus dolutegravir + emtricitabine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (DTG+F/TDF) in treatment-naive people with HIV-1/hepatitis B virus (HBV) coinfection. The design of the phase 3 randomized ALLIANCE study (GS-US-380-4458; NCT03547908) has been described previously. In brief, participants were aged ≥18 years, with HIV-1 RNA ≥500 copies/mL and HBV DNA ≥2000 IU/mL. All participants provided written informed consent, and the study was undertaken in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki and following site-specific ethical approval. During the randomized phase, participants continued masked treatment until the final participant had completed their Week 96 assessment. Participants were then requested to attend an end-of-masked-treatment visit. At this visit, participants from both treatment groups could enter the OLE phase and receive B/F/TAF for a further 48 weeks. Efficacy findings for the 48-week OLE phase will be reported separately.","The paper describes the ALLIANCE study as a phase 3 randomized clinical trial, confirming that the patients were part of a clinical trial."
40857111,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,208,"In total, 208 participants received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in either phase and had ≥1 on-treatment, postbaseline HIV-1 RNA and HBV DNA result; 119 of these participants only received B/F/TAF while 89 received DTG + F/TDF in the randomized phase, then received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in the OLE phase.","The paper states that 208 participants received at least one dose of the treatment and had postbaseline HIV-1 RNA and HBV DNA results, indicating the number of individuals whose samples were obtained for sequencing."
40857111,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"ALLIANCE is a phase 3 randomized study of bibtegravir/emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide (B/F/TAF) versus dolutegravir + emtricitabine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (DTG+F/TDF) in treatment-naive people with HIV-1/hepatitis B virus (HBV) coinfection. In total, 208 participants received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in either phase and had ≥1 on-treatment, postbaseline HIV-1 RNA and HBV DNA result; 119 of these participants only received B/F/TAF while 89 received  DTG + F/TDF in the randomized phase, then received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in the OLE phase. This subanalysis reports HIV-1 resistance results to the end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF, n = 122) and to study end (B/F/TAF, n = 119; DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF, n = 89)",The paper mention that the study included treatment-naive  people with HIV-1/hepatitis B virus (HBV) coinfection and they all were  received DTG+F/TDF or B/F/TAF regimens.
40857111,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, INSTI","B/F/TAF, bitegravir/emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide; DTG, dolutegravir; F/TDF, emtricitabine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate; NNRTI, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor; NRTI, nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitor; PI, protease inhibitor; R, resistance; RAM, resistance-associated mutation; RT, reverse transcriptase; TAM, thymidine analog mutation. Background: ALLIANCE is a phase 3 randomized study of bibtegravir/emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide (B/F/TAF) versus dolutegravir + emtricitabine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (DTG+F/TDF) in treatment-naive people with HIV-1/hepatitis B virus (HBV) coinfection. In total, 208 participants received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in either phase and had ≥1 on-treatment, postbaseline HIV-1 RNA and HBV DNA result; 119 of these participants only received B/F/TAF while 89 received DTG + F/TDF in the randomized phase, then received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in the OLE phase.  This subanalysis reports HIV-1 resistance results to the end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF, n = 122) and to study end (B/F/TAF, n = 119; DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF, n = 89)","The paper mentions that participants received B/F/TAF or DTG+F/TDF, which include NRTI and integrase inhibitor classes."
40857111,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Bictegravir, Emtricitabine, Tenofovir Alafenamide, Dolutegral, Emtricitabine, Tenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate","B/F/TAF, bitegravir/emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide; DTG, dolutegravir; F/TDF, emtricitabine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate; NNRTI, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor; NRTI, nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitor; PI, protease inhibitor; R, resistance; RAM, resistance-associated mutation; RT, reverse transcriptase; TAM, thymidine analog mutation. Background: ALLIANCE is a phase 3 randomized study of bibtegravir/emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide (B/F/TAF) versus dolutegravir + emtricitabine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (DTG+F/TDF) in treatment-naive people with HIV-1/hepatitis B virus (HBV) coinfection. In total, 208 participants received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in either phase and had ≥1 on-treatment, postbaseline HIV-1 RNA and HBV DNA result; 119 of these participants only received B/F/TAF while 89 received DTG + F/TDF in the randomized phase, then received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in the OLE phase.  This subanalysis reports HIV-1 resistance results to the end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF, n = 122) and to study end (B/F/TAF, n = 119; DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF, n = 89)","The paper mentions that participants received B/F/TAF or DTG+F/TDF, which include tenofovir, emtricitabine, dolutegravir, and bictegravir."
40857111,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The design of the phase 3 randomized ALLIANCE study (GS-US-380-4458; NCT03547908) has been described previously. In brief, participants were aged ≥18 years, with HIV-1 RNA ≥500 copies/mL and HBV DNA ≥2000 IU/mL. All participants provided written informed consent, and the study was undertaken in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki and following site-specific ethical approval. During the randomized phase, participants continued masked treatment until the final participant had completed their Week 96 assessment. Participants were then requested to attend an end-of-masked-treatment visit. At this visit, participants from both treatment groups could enter the OLE phase and receive B/F/TAF for a further 48 weeks. Efficacy findings for the 48-week OLE phase will be reported separately. Assessments of HIV-1 viral load, HIV-1 subtype, and of baseline and postbaseline HIV-1 resistance were performed as described previously.[15] The final RAP for the masked phase and at study end included all participants who experienced VF (HIV-1 RNA ≥200 copies/mL) and did not subsequently achieve resuppression (HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/m/L) on study drugs. There were 3 final RAP groups based on study treatment in the different phases of the study: (1) participants who received DTG+F/TDF in the randomized phase, analyzed through end of masked treatment; (2) participants who received B/F 1TAF in the randomized phase, irrespective of receiving B/F/TAF in the OLE phase, analyzed through end of study; and (3) participants who received DTG+F/TDF in the randomized phase and then received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in the OLE phase, analyzed from study start through end of study. Fisher exact test was used to compare the proportions of participants with HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL at end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF recipients) or at study end (B/F/TAF recipients), by virologic categories, and the proportions of participants meeting the criteria for inclusion in the final RAP groups. .",The paper does not mention making sequences publicly available.
40872801,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"No, because it does not mention about sequecing from patient samples","A systematic literature review was conducted from February to May 2025 following PRISMA standards [11], including original studies that were conducted in Tanzania involving participants receiving ART published in English by the end of December 2024. The systematic review protocol was registered in the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (PROSPERO) on 7 January 2025 under the registration number CRD42025606698.",The paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples. It mentions a systematic literature review of existing studies involving patients receiving ART in Tanzania but does not detail original sequencing work on patient samples.
40872801,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The emergence and spread of HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) pose a threat to current and future treatment options. To inform policy, this review aimed to determine the magnitude and patterns of DRMs in patients on ART in Tanzania.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on the prevalence of drug resistance mutations and HIV-1 subtypes in patients on ART in Tanzania.
40872801,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.,"A systematic literature review was conducted from February to May 2025 following PRISMA standards [11], including original studies that were conducted in Tanzania involving participants receiving ART published in English by the end of December 2024. The systematic review protocol was registered in the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (PROSPERO) on 7 January 2025 under the registration number CRD42025606698.","The paper does not provide individual GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates, as it is a systematic review and meta-analysis of existing studies."
40872801,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,The paper does not specify which HIV genes were sequenced.,"A systematic literature review was conducted from February to May 2025 following PRISMA standards [11], including original studies that were conducted in Tanzania involving participants receiving ART published in English by the end of December 2024. The systematic review protocol was registered in the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (PROSPERO) on 7 January 2025 under the registration number CRD42025606698.","The paper does not mention specific HIV genes that were sequenced, as it focuses on the prevalence of drug resistance mutations and HIV-1 subtypes."
40872801,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper reports samples from Tanzania.,"The emergence and spread of HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) pose a threat to current and future treatment options. To inform policy, this review aimed to determine the magnitude and patterns of DRMs in patients on ART in Tanzania. A systematic literature search was conducted in MEDLINE through PubMed, Embase, and CINAHL up to December 2024.",The paper specifically focuses on studies conducted in Tanzania and involving participants receiving ART in Tanzania.
40872801,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2005-2024,"A systematic literature review was conducted from February to May 2025 following PRISMA standards [11], including original studies that were conducted in Tanzania involving participants receiving ART published in English by the end of December 2024. A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed. The earliest sampling year was 2005, and the latest study year was 2024, although studies that were fetched were published by the end of 2025.","he paper mentions the inclusion of studies conducted between 2005 and 2024, which implies that the  samples were obtained during these years."
40872801,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The paper does not specify the sequencing method used.,"A systematic literature review was conducted from February to May 2025 following PRISMA standards [11], including original studies that were conducted in Tanzania involving participants receiving ART published in English by the end of December 2024. The systematic review protocol was registered in the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (PROSPERO) on 7 January 2025 under the registration number CRD42025606698.","The paper does not mention the specific sequencing methods used in the studies it reviews, as it focuses on the outcomes rather than the methodologies."
40872801,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,The paper does not mention whether samples were cloned prior to sequencing.,"A systematic literature review was conducted from February to May 2025 following PRISMA standards [11], including original studies that were conducted in Tanzania involving participants receiving ART published in English by the end of December 2024. The systematic review protocol was registered in the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (PROSPERO) on 7 January 2025 under the registration number CRD42025606698.","The paper does not discuss the cloning of samples prior to sequencing, as it does not detail the laboratory procedures of the included studies."
40872801,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,The paper does not specify the type of samples that were sequenced.,"A systematic literature review was conducted from February to May 2025 following PRISMA standards [11], including original studies that were conducted in Tanzania involving participants receiving ART published in English by the end of December 2024. The systematic review protocol was registered in the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (PROSPERO) on 7 January 2025 under the registration number CRD42025606698.","The paper does not mention the type of samples (e.g., plasma, PBMCs) that were sequenced, as it focuses on the prevalence of drug resistance mutations."
40872801,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"The systematic literature search was carried out between February and March 2025 on online databases including MEDLINE through PubMed, Embase, and CINAHL, while Google Scholar was used to identify grey literature and would not be identified by other databases. The concepts ""Drug Resistance Mutations"", ""Drug Resistance"", ""ART resistance"", ""HIV-1 Subtypes"", ""HIV Subtypes"", ""HIV"", and ""Tanzania"" were used as key concepts alongside related search terms to construct the search strategy. Virological failure, when available, was expressed as the total number of individuals with a viral load higher than the set virological failure threshold to the total number of individuals in whom viral load was assessed. Some studies followed participants longitudinally, assessing virological failure as well as DRMs prevalence, and other studies enrolled participants who already had virological failure.","The paper mentions the inclusion of studies that reported outcomes for individuals with virological failure, and it discusses the prevalence of drug resistance mutations in this population."
40872801,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"All included studies were observational studies, including a total of 9685 participants on ART, whereby 6677 (68.94%) were women. Eight (34.78%) of included studies involved child participants, whereby a total of 2320 (23.95%) children were enrolled. The characteristics of studies that were included with their respective participants are summarized in Table 1.","The paper reports  the study is a systematic review and meta-analysis of existing observational studies, not clinical trials."
40872801,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,9685,"All included studies were observational studies, including a total of 9685 participants on ART, whereby 6677 (68.94%) were women. Eight (34.78%) of included studies involved child participants, whereby a total of 2320 (23.95%) children were enrolled. The characteristics of studies that were included with their respective participants are summarized in Table 1.",The paper mentions that a total of 9685 participants on ART were included in the meta-analysis of the studies It summarizes.
40872801,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"The emergence and spread of HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) pose a threat to current and future treatment options. To inform policy, this review aimed to determine the magnitude and patterns of DRMs in patients on ART in Tanzania. A systematic literature search was conducted in MEDLINE through PubMed, Embase, and CINAHL up to December 2024. A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed. The characteristics of studies that were included with their respective participants are summarized in Table 1.All included studies were observational studies, including a total of 9685 participants on ART, whereby 6677 (68.94%) were women. Eight (34.78%) of included studies involved child participants, whereby a total of 2320 (23.95%) children were enrolled. The characteristics of studies that were included with their respective participants are summarized in Table 1.","The paper mentions a systematic review and meta-analysis of studies involving participants receiving ART, indicating that some had previously received ARV drugs."
40872801,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,The paper does not specify the drug classes received prior to sample sequencing.,"The emergence and spread of HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) pose a threat to current and future treatment options. To inform policy, this review aimed to determine the magnitude and patterns of DRMs in patients on ART in Tanzania. A systematic literature search was conducted in MEDLINE through PubMed, Embase, and CINAHL up to December 2024. A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed. The characteristics of studies that were included with their respective participants are summarized in Table 1.All included studies were observational studies, including a total of 9685 participants on ART, whereby 6677 (68.94%) were women. Eight (34.78%) of included studies involved child participants, whereby a total of 2320 (23.95%) children were enrolled. The characteristics of studies that were included with their respective participants are summarized in Table 1.","The paper does not detail the specific drug classes received by individuals before sample sequencing, as it focuses on the outcomes of the included studies."
40872801,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,The paper does not specify the drugs received prior to sample sequencing.,"The emergence and spread of HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) pose a threat to current and future treatment options. To inform policy, this review aimed to determine the magnitude and patterns of DRMs in patients on ART in Tanzania. A systematic literature search was conducted in MEDLINE through PubMed, Embase, and CINAHL up to December 2024. A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed. The characteristics of studies that were included with their respective participants are summarized in Table 1.All included studies were observational studies, including a total of 9685 participants on ART, whereby 6677 (68.94%) were women. Eight (34.78%) of included studies involved child participants, whereby a total of 2320 (23.95%) children were enrolled. The characteristics of studies that were included with their respective participants are summarized in Table 1.","The paper does not mention the specific drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing, as it focuses on the prevalence of drug resistance mutations across various studies."
40872801,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,Data supporting the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon request.,"The paper mentions that data supporting the findings are available from the corresponding author upon request, which implies that they are not publicly available."
40938120,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"Yes, the paper reports a total of 1,048 eligible pol sequences analyzed from patient samples.","A total of 1,048 eligible pol sequences were analyzed.","The paper states that 1,048 pol sequences were analyzed, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
40938120,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No.,"Emerging pretreatment drug resistance (PDR) significantly reduces the effectiveness of HIV antiretroviral therapy (ART). This study assessed the prevalence, associated factors, and transmission networks of PDR among newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals in Guangxi, China, from 2019 to 2022. A cross-sectional study was conducted between 2019 and 2022 in Nanning, Guangxi, involving 1,260 newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals with HIV. PDR levels and mutations were identified using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. Multivariable logistic regression models were employed to identify factors associated with PDR and to cluster the molecular network. A total of 1,048 eligible pol sequences were analyzed. The overall prevalence of PDR was 8.4%, with non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) being the most commonly affected drug class. The most prevalent NNRTI-associated mutation was E138A (2.4%). High-level resistance was primarily observed to efavirenz and nevirapine. The CRF08_BC subtype exhibited significant clustering of PDR-related sequences. Those individuals diagnosed in 2022 were more likely to have PDR. Transmission networks clustering was significantly associated with CRF01_AE and CRF08_BC subtypes, older age, and heterosexual transmission. This study identified a moderate prevalence of PDR among newly diagnosed HIV patients in Guangxi, primarily driven by NNRTI-associated resistance mutations. The pronounced clustering of PDR in the CRF08_BC subtype highlights the need for subtype-specific surveillance and intervention strategies. To improve treatment outcomes and constrain the spread of resistance, targeted educational programs on ART adherence and drug resistance awareness should be prioritized, especially among older adults and individuals infected through heterosexual contacts, alongside enhanced molecular monitoring.",The paper focuses on the prevalence and analysis of drug resistance mutations rather than specific in vitro susceptibility testing data.
40938120,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates are PX133190-PX134237.,HIV-1 sequence data generated in this study have been deposited in GenBank and are available under accession numbers PX133190-PX134237.,The paper explicitly mentions that the sequence data have been deposited in GenBank under these accession numbers.
40938120,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"The paper reports sequencing of the pol gene, covering the full-length protease (PR) and the first 299 codons of the reverse transcriptase (RT) gene.","An in-house RT nested PCR was used to amplify a 1,061 bp fragment of the pol gene, covering the full-length protease (PR, 99 codons) and the first 299 codons of the RT gene.","The paper specifies that a fragment of the pol gene, which includes sequences for protease and reverse transcriptase, was amplified and sequenced."
40938120,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained from China.,"Molecular epidemiology and pretreatment drug resistance of HIV-1 among newly diagnosed individuals in Nanning City, Guangxi, China. Emerging pretreatment drug resistance (PDR) significantly reduces the effectiveness of HIV antiretroviral therapy (ART). This study assessed the prevalence, associated factors, and transmission networks of PDR among newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals in Guangxi, China, from 2019 to 2022.","The study was conducted in Nanning City, Guangxi, China, and the samples were from newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals in this region."
40938120,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained from 2019 to 2022.,"A cross-sectional study was conducted between 2019 and 2022 in Nanning, Guangxi, involving 1,260 newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals with HIV.","The paper states that the study was conducted between 2019 and 2022, during which the samples were collected."
40938120,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The sequencing was performed using the Applied Biosystems 3730XL Genetic Analyzer.,"PCR products were confirmed by agarose gel electrophoresis, and positive amplicons were sequenced by Sangon Biotech (Shanghai, China) using the Applied Biosystems 3730XL Genetic Analyzer.",The paper specifies that the sequencing was done using the Applied Biosystems 3730XL Genetic Analyzer.
40938120,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,"No, the samples were not cloned prior to sequencing.","PCR products were confirmed by agarose gel electrophoresis, and positive amplicons were sequenced by Sangon Biotech (Shanghai, China) using the Applied Biosystems 3730XL Genetic Analyzer. Raw chromatogram data were assembled and cleaned using Sequencher 5.4.6.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40938120,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples were sequenced.,"HIV-1 RNA was extracted from plasma samples using the High Pure Viral RNA Kit (Roche, Germany).",The paper mentions that HIV-1 RNA was extracted from plasma samples for sequencing.
40938120,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"No, all individuals were newly diagnosed and ART-naive, so they had not experienced virological failure.",The participant inclusion criteria were as follows: (i) aged 18 years or older; (ii) newly diagnosed with HIV and ART naive;,"The study focused on newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals, not those with virological failure."
40938120,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"No, the study was a cross-sectional survey and not a clinical trial.","A cross-sectional study was conducted between 2019 and 2022 in Nanning, Guangxi, involving 1,260 newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals with HIV. The participant inclusion criteria were as follows: (i) aged 18 years or older; (ii) newly diagnosed with HIV and ART-naive; (iii) residence in Nanning for more than three months before diagnosis; and (iv) ability to provide verbal and written informed consent in Mandarin.","The study describes a cross-sectional survey, not a clinical trial."
40938120,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,"Samples were obtained from 1,260 individuals for HIV sequencing.","In this study, we enrolled 1,260 newly diagnosed HIV-infected individuals.","The paper states that 1,260 newly diagnosed individuals were enrolled, and the sequences were obtained from them."
40938120,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"No, the individuals were ART-naive, meaning they had not received ARV drugs before.","Emerging pretreatment drug resistance (PDR) significantly reduces the effectiveness of HIV antiretroviral therapy (ART). This study assessed the prevalence, associated factors, and transmission networks of PDR among newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals in Guangxi, China, from 2019 to 2022. The participant inclusion criteria were as follows: (i) aged 18 years or older; (ii) newly diagnosed with HIV and ART naive;",The inclusion of  ART naïve individuals indicates that they have not received ARV drugs before.
40938120,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"No individuals had received ARV drugs before sample sequencing, as they were newly diagnosed and ART-naive.","Emerging pretreatment drug resistance (PDR) significantly reduces the effectiveness of HIV antiretroviral therapy (ART). This study assessed the prevalence, associated factors, and transmission networks of PDR among newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals in Guangxi, China, from 2019 to 2022. The participant inclusion criteria were as follows: (i) aged 18 years or older; (ii) newly diagnosed with HIV and ART naive;",The inclusion of  ART naïve individuals indicates that they have not received ARV drugs before.
40938120,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"No individuals had received ARV drugs before sample sequencing, as they were newly diagnosed and ART-naive.","Emerging pretreatment drug resistance (PDR) significantly reduces the effectiveness of HIV antiretroviral therapy (ART). This study assessed the prevalence, associated factors, and transmission networks of PDR among newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals in Guangxi, China, from 2019 to 2022. The participant inclusion criteria were as follows: (i) aged 18 years or older; (ii) newly diagnosed with HIV and ART naïve;",The inclusion of  ART naïve individuals indicates that they have not received ARV drugs before.
40938120,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,"Yes, the sequences were deposited in GenBank and are publicly available.",HIV-1 sequence data generated in this study have been deposited in GenBank and are available under accession numbers PX133190-PX134237.,"The paper mentions that the sequence data have been deposited in GenBank, indicating that they are publicly available."
40938996,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"No, the paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples.","We previously reported that mutations in the viral nucleocapsid (NC) are selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir (DTG). Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance.","The paper discusses the sequencing and mutation analysis of HIV-1 in the context of laboratory studies using cell lines and primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, not directly from patient samples."
40938996,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data.","Here, we demonstrated that INSTI resistance conferred by NC mutations is closely associated with accelerated early postentry events in the HIV-1 replication cycle. Notably, while these NC mutations do not have a major effect on virus assembly or infectivity, they significantly shorten the time between completion of reverse transcription/uncoating and viral DNA integration. These results demonstrate that the NC mutations narrow the time window during which INSTIs can effectively inhibit integration, offering a novel mechanism for INSTI resistance.","The paper focuses on the mechanistic studies of HIV-1 nucleocapsid mutations and their effects on integrase strand transfer inhibitors, but it does not include specific in vitro drug susceptibility assays or data."
40938996,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.,"We previously reported that mutations in the viral nucleocapsid (NC) are selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir (DTG). Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance.","The paper does not mention any specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that they were not provided."
40938996,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"The paper reports sequencing of HIV genes in the Env, NC, and IN regions.","We previously reported that mutations in the viral nucleocapsid (NC) are selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir (DTG). Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance.","The paper mentions that mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, nucleocapsid (NC), and occasionally integrase (IN) are sequenced and analyzed."
40938996,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,No country was listed,"We previously reported that mutations in the viral nucleocapsid (NC) are selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir (DTG). Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance.","The paper does not specify any countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained, indicating that this information is not provided."
40938996,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not specify the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained.,"Here, we demonstrate that INSTI resistance conferred by NC mutations is closely associated with accelerated early postentry events in the HIV-1 replication cycle. To investigate which early step(s) in the virus replication cycle are accelerated by the NC mutants, we analyzed the impact of the prototype NC-G19S mutation on the kinetics of reverse transcription, integration, and 2-LTR circle formation by quantitative PCR (qPCR) (Fig. 3, C to E). In addition to showing more viral DNA integration at early time points relative to the WT, NC-G19S also shows a subtle but statistically significant shift in integration out of SPADs and into LADs. In summary, this study provides insights into the role of NC in the HIV-1 replication cycle and underscores the importance of mutations outside drug-target genes in the development of INSTI resistance.","The paper does not mention the specific years during which the samples were obtained, indicating that this information is not provided."
40938996,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The paper used Sanger sequcening method for HIV genomes.,"The features that distinguish variants determined by Sanger sequencing from those identified based on single-genome sequencing are that Sanger sequencing detects majority variants exhibiting resistance to a concentration of integrase inhibitors at the 5-h time point (not shown) on day 7 postinfection, which would be compatible with the presence of the wild-type virus and much lower frequencies of resistance-associated mutations. Therefore, dual-infection experiments with genetically distinct HIV-1 variants are necessary to identify mutations compensating for defects in integrase or Gag processing most clearly, as shown here. Mutations in the PQ loop and the C-terminal domain of NC, which interact with IN or integrate into the host cell, are present in HIV-1NL4 3 in the presence of DTG or Y181C or Liov-kw3-334ugu-3D3LA-1. We demonstrated that a numerically greater burden of mutations in NC occurs in the presence of DTG than in the presence of RPV, suggesting that activation of packaged NC, which promotes DNA integration, is needed to overcome INSTI inhibition of IN",The paper mentioned the use of Sanger sequencing for lab experiments.
40938996,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,"No, samples were not cloned prior to sequencing.","We previously reported that mutations in the viral nucleocapsid (NC) are selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir (DTG). Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance. We find that the NC-G19S mutation is selected in the presence of DTG in late-stage primary human CD4+ T cells, indicating that NC mutations can confer INSTI resistance in primary T cells.","The paper does not mention any process of cloning samples before sequencing, indicating that this was not done."
40938996,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,The paper sequenced viral genomes from HIV-1-infected primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) and other studies reports from the SupT1 T cell line.,"Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance. NC-G19S does not alter the efficiency or kinetics of uncoating in the nucleus. We previously demonstrated that the NC mutations selected in the presence of DTG, most of which cluster in the zinc finger domains (fig. S2A), do not affect single-cycle, cell-free infectivity A, C, D). We previously reported that HIV-1 is able to infect nondividing primary human CD4 T cells in the presence of DTG. To validate these observations with a different assay system, we performed TOA analysis using HeLa cells and a VSV-G pseudotyped HIV-1 encoding an mScarlet fluorescent reporter (fig. S5). Consistent with the data presented in Fig. 4A and fig. S5, NC-G19S did not show a statistically significant difference in the time to loss of sensitivity to the RT inhibitor RPV or the CA inhibitor LEN but showed an increase in the kinetics of viral DNA integration, resulting in a shorter time between completion of reverse transcription or uncoating and integration. Viral inputs were normalized by RT activity. SupT1 or PHA-stimulated primary CD4 T cells (2.0 106 cells) were incubated with the RT-normalized VSV-G-pseudotyped nanoLuc reporter virus at 37 °C for 2 hours.","The paper mentions sequencing in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) List isolation method) and cultured SupT1 T cells (the SupT1huR5 T cell line) homogenous and were used in a mixed population, continuous cell line (the TZM-bl indicator cell line)."
40938996,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"No, sequences were not obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen.","We previously reported that mutations in the viral nucleocapsid (NC) are selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir (DTG). Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance. NC-G19S does not alter the efficiency or kinetics of uncoating in the nucleus. We previously demonstrated that the NC mutations selected in the presence of DTG, most of which cluster in the zinc finger domains (fig. S2A), do not affect single-cycle, cell-free infectivity A, C, D). We previously reported that HIV-1 is able to infect nondividing primary human CD4 T cells in the presence of DTG. To validate these observations with a different assay system, we performed TOA analysis using HeLa cells and a VSV-G pseudotyped HIV-1 encoding an mScarlet fluorescent reporter (fig. S5). Consistent with the data presented in Fig. 4A and fig. S5, NC-G19S did not show a statistically significant difference in the time to loss of sensitivity to the RT inhibitor RPV or the CA inhibitor LEN but showed an increase in the kinetics of viral DNA integration, resulting in a shorter time between completion of reverse transcription or uncoating and integration. Viral inputs were normalized by RT activity. SupT1 or PHA-stimulated primary CD4 T cells (2.0 106 cells) were incubated with the RT-normalized VSV-G-pseudotyped nanoLuc reporter virus at 37 °C for 2 hours.16 Transfected cells (1.5 105 cells) were plated in 96-well flat-bottom plates and incubated at 37 °C in the presence of a range of concentrations of DTG (0 to 300 nM). Aliquots of supernatants were collected to measure RT activity, and cells were split 1:3 every other day with fresh drug and medium. During each serial passage, 25% of the infected cell culture was cocultured with uninfected SupT1huR5 T cells in a separate culture to monitor viral replication kinetics in the presence of DTG, as shown previously (49). At the indicated time points, genomic DNA was extracted from infected cells using the DNeasy Blood and Tissue Minikit (QIAGEN), and the Gag-Pol and Env-Nef coding regions were amplified by PrimeSTAR GXL DNA polymerase (Takara Bio) and sequenced by Sanger or Oxford Nanopore methods (Psomagen) using previously described primers (98, 99).","The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure, focusing instead on laboratory studies and resistance mechanisms."
40938996,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"No, the study did not involve patients in a clinical trial.","We previously reported that mutations in the viral nucleocapsid (NC) are selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir (DTG). Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance. NC-G19S does not alter the efficiency or kinetics of uncoating in the nucleus. We previously demonstrated that the NC mutations selected in the presence of DTG, most of which cluster in the zinc finger domains (fig. S2A), do not affect single-cycle, cell-free infectivity A, C, D). We previously reported that HIV-1 is able to infect nondividing primary human CD4 T cells in the presence of DTG. To validate these observations with a different assay system, we performed TOA analysis using HeLa cells and a VSV-G pseudotyped HIV-1 encoding an mScarlet fluorescent reporter (fig. S5). Consistent with the data presented in Fig. 4A and fig. S5, NC-G19S did not show a statistically significant difference in the time to loss of sensitivity to the RT inhibitor RPV or the CA inhibitor LEN but showed an increase in the kinetics of viral DNA integration, resulting in a shorter time between completion of reverse transcription or uncoating and integration. Viral inputs were normalized by RT activity. SupT1 or PHA-stimulated primary CD4 T cells (2.0 106 cells) were incubated with the RT-normalized VSV-G-pseudotyped nanoLuc reporter virus at 37 °C for 2 hours.","The paper does not mention any clinical trials involving patients, focusing instead on laboratory experiments and analyses."
40938996,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,The paper does not specify the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing.,"We previously reported that mutations in the viral nucleocapsid (NC) are selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir (DTG). Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance. NC-G19S does not alter the efficiency or kinetics of uncoating in the nucleus. We previously demonstrated that the NC mutations selected in the presence of DTG, most of which cluster in the zinc finger domains (fig. S2A), do not affect single-cycle, cell-free infectivity A, C, D). We previously reported that HIV-1 is able to infect nondividing primary human CD4 T cells in the presence of DTG. To validate these observations with a different assay system, we performed TOA analysis using HeLa cells and a VSV-G pseudotyped HIV-1 encoding an mScarlet fluorescent reporter (fig. S5). Consistent with the data presented in Fig. 4A and fig. S5, NC-G19S did not show a statistically significant difference in the time to loss of sensitivity to the RT inhibitor RPV or the CA inhibitor LEN but showed an increase in the kinetics of viral DNA integration, resulting in a shorter time between completion of reverse transcription or uncoating and integration. Viral inputs were normalized by RT activity. SupT1 or PHA-stimulated primary CD4 T cells (2.0 106 cells) were incubated with the RT-normalized VSV-G-pseudotyped nanoLuc reporter virus at 37 °C for 2 hours.16 Transfected cells (1.5 105 cells) were plated in 96-well flat-bottom plates and incubated at 37 °C in the presence of a range of concentrations of DTG (0 to 300 nM). Aliquots of supernatants were collected to measure RT activity, and cells were split 1:3 every other day with fresh drug and medium. During each serial passage, 25% of the infected cell culture was cocultured with uninfected SupT1huR5 T cells in a separate culture to monitor viral replication kinetics in the presence of DTG, as shown previously (49). At the indicated time points, genomic DNA was extracted from infected cells using the DNeasy Blood and Tissue Minikit (QIAGEN), and the Gag-Pol and Env-Nef coding regions were amplified by PrimeSTAR GXL DNA polymerase (Takara Bio) and sequenced by Sanger or Oxford Nanopore methods (Psomagen) using previously described primers (98, 99). HIV-1 acquires mutations in Env, NC, and IN in the presence of DTG in primary T cells. Our previously reported DTG selection experiments were performed in immortalized T cell lines (49). To gain further insights into INSTI resistance pathways under more physiologically relevant conditions, we conducted long-term propagation experiments with increasing DTG concentrations in peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs). We infected PBMCs with three different HIV-1 strains: the subtype B, X4-tropic, NL4-3 strain (Fig. 5A); the subtype B, R5-tropic, transmitted-founder isolate CH077 (Fig. 5B); and the subtype C, R5-tropic, transmitted-founder strain CH185 (Fig. 5C). To identify the genetic changes responsible for DTG resistance, we extracted genomic DNA from infected cells and performed sequencing analysis across the HIV-1 genome. Consistent with our previous study using the SupT1 T cell line (49), the NL4-3 strain sequentially acquired multiple Env mutations and mutations in NC (NC-N27I) and IN (IN-S153Y and IN-D167H). Similarly, the CH077 molecular clone acquired three Env mutations (V208A, R577K, and D743N), NC-N27K, and IN-T122I. CH185 acquired and subsequently lost NC-A30T and acquired two Env mutations (A533V and L764M), another NC mutation (NC-D48N), and two IN mutations (IN-E163K and R263K). We did not identify any mutations in the 3 0 PPT G-tract region (39-44) in the viral strains tested here. Inactivating mutations in Vpu were also frequently detected, consistent with previous reports from us and others (47, 74, 75).","The paper does not specify the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing, focusing instead on laboratory studies and mutational analyses."
40938996,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"No, the paper does not report sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.","We previously reported that mutations in the viral nucleocapsid (NC) are selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir (DTG). Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance. NC-G19S does not alter the efficiency or kinetics of uncoating in the nucleus. We previously demonstrated that the NC mutations selected in the presence of DTG, most of which cluster in the zinc finger domains (fig. S2A), do not affect single-cycle, cell-free infectivity A, C, D). We previously reported that HIV-1 is able to infect nondividing primary human CD4 T cells in the presence of DTG. To validate these observations with a different assay system, we performed TOA analysis using HeLa cells and a VSV-G pseudotyped HIV-1 encoding an mScarlet fluorescent reporter (fig. S5). Consistent with the data presented in Fig. 4A and fig. S5, NC-G19S did not show a statistically significant difference in the time to loss of sensitivity to the RT inhibitor RPV or the CA inhibitor LEN but showed an increase in the kinetics of viral DNA integration, resulting in a shorter time between completion of reverse transcription or uncoating and integration. Viral inputs were normalized by RT activity. SupT1 or PHA-stimulated primary CD4 T cells (2.0 106 cells) were incubated with the RT-normalized VSV-G-pseudotyped nanoLuc reporter virus at 37 °C for 2 hours.","The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs, focusing instead on laboratory studies and resistance mechanisms."
