41012586,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,The paper does not report any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing.,"We compared three regimen groups: dual DTG + DRV/b (DODA), DTG + DRV/b plus an additional antiretroviral drug (DODA + Other), and regimens excluding DTG + DRV/b (NO-DODA). Virological failure (VF) was defined as ≥ 2 HIV-RNA values ≥ 50 copies/mL or 1 ≥ 1000 copies/mL. Mixed-effects logistic regression was used to assess VF, adjusting for antiretroviral therapy (ART) duration, age, number of fully active drugs, sex at birth, and nadir CD4+. Individuals could switch regimens during follow-up. Results: Among 249 4DR-PWH (median follow-up: 8.7 years), 844 ART regimens were analyzed: 72 (8.5%) DODA, 264 (31.3%) DODA + Other, and 508 (60.2%) NO-DODA. Compared to NO-DODA, the odds of VF were 77% and 35.9% lower with DODA and DODA + Other, respectively. Notably, in the DODA group, DTG and DRV/b were fully active in only 63.9% and 47.2% of the cases, respectively. Conclusions: DTG + DRV/b regimens were associated with a significantly lower risk of virological failure, even when drug activity was partial. This strategy remains a valuable option for managing multi-drug-resistant HIV.","The paper does not mention any individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing, focusing instead on treatment regimens."
41012586,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,The paper does not report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.,"We compared three regimen groups: dual DTG + DRV/b (DODA), DTG + DRV/b plus an additional antiretroviral drug (DODA + Other), and regimens excluding DTG + DRV/b (NO-DODA). Virological failure (VF) was defined as ≥ 2 HIV-RNA values ≥ 50 copies/mL or 1 ≥ 1000 copies/mL. Mixed-effects logistic regression was used to assess VF, adjusting for antiretroviral therapy (ART) duration, age, number of fully active drugs, sex at birth, and nadir CD4+. Individuals could switch regimens during follow-up. Results: Among 249 4DR-PWH (median follow-up: 8.7 years), 844 ART regimens were analyzed: 72 (8.5%) DODA, 264 (31.3%) DODA + Other, and 508 (60.2%) NO-DODA. Compared to NO-DODA, the odds of VF were 77% and 35.9% lower with DODA and DODA + Other, respectively. Notably, in the DODA group, DTG and DRV/b were fully active in only 63.9% and 47.2% of the cases, respectively. Conclusions: DTG + DRV/b regimens were associated with a significantly lower risk of virological failure, even when drug activity was partial. This strategy remains a valuable option for managing multi-drug-resistant HIV.",The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.
41012586,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,The paper does not specify the drug classes received by individuals before sequencing.,"We compared three regimen groups: dual DTG + DRV/b (DODA), DTG + DRV/b plus an additional antiretroviral drug (DODA + Other), and regimens excluding DTG + DRV/b (NO-DODA). Virological failure (VF) was defined as ≥ 2 HIV-RNA values ≥ 50 copies/mL or 1 ≥ 1000 copies/mL. Mixed-effects logistic regression was used to assess VF, adjusting for antiretroviral therapy (ART) duration, age, number of fully active drugs, sex at birth, and nadir CD4+. Individuals could switch regimens during follow-up. Results: Among 249 4DR-PWH (median follow-up: 8.7 years), 844 ART regimens were analyzed: 72 (8.5%) DODA, 264 (31.3%) DODA + Other, and 508 (60.2%) NO-DODA. Compared to NO-DODA, the odds of VF were 77% and 35.9% lower with DODA and DODA + Other, respectively. Notably, in the DODA group, DTG and DRV/b were fully active in only 63.9% and 47.2% of the cases, respectively. Conclusions: DTG + DRV/b regimens were associated with a significantly lower risk of virological failure, even when drug activity was partial. This strategy remains a valuable option for managing multi-drug-resistant HIV.","The paper does not specify any drug classes received before sequencing, focusing instead on the treatment regimens."
41012586,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,The paper does not report which specific drugs were received by individuals before sequencing.,"We compared three regimen groups: dual DTG + DRV/b (DODA), DTG + DRV/b plus an additional antiretroviral drug (DODA + Other), and regimens excluding DTG + DRV/b (NO-DODA). Virological failure (VF) was defined as ≥ 2 HIV-RNA values ≥ 50 copies/mL or 1 ≥ 1000 copies/mL. Mixed-effects logistic regression was used to assess VF, adjusting for antiretroviral therapy (ART) duration, age, number of fully active drugs, sex at birth, and nadir CD4+. Individuals could switch regimens during follow-up. Results: Among 249 4DR-PWH (median follow-up: 8.7 years), 844 ART regimens were analyzed: 72 (8.5%) DODA, 264 (31.3%) DODA + Other, and 508 (60.2%) NO-DODA. Compared to NO-DODA, the odds of VF were 77% and 35.9% lower with DODA and DODA + Other, respectively. Notably, in the DODA group, DTG and DRV/b were fully active in only 63.9% and 47.2% of the cases, respectively. Conclusions: DTG + DRV/b regimens were associated with a significantly lower risk of virological failure, even when drug activity was partial. This strategy remains a valuable option for managing multi-drug-resistant HIV.","The paper does not specify any drugs received before sequencing, focusing instead on treatment outcomes."
41012586,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,"The paper states that data are available on request, but does not confirm that the sequences were made publicly available.","In conclusion, among 4DR-PWH, the combination of DRV/b and DTG was associated with a lower probability of virological failure, even after adjusting for the number of fully active drugs. This combination remains highly effective compared to when it is not used, even when not fully supported by the activity of both drugs, or when combined with other antiretrovirals. The use of this combination in this setting—particularly the component involving protease inhibitors—is likely to decline in the future. New therapeutic options are emerging, including broadly neutralizing antibodies (bNAbs) and third-generation integrase inhibitors. These, together with the increasing availability and clinical use of recently approved drugs such as fostemsavir and lenacapavir, are collectively reshaping the treatment landscape. Nonetheless, the high efficacy of DTG and DRV/b combination remains highly relevant, particularly for fragile patients with multi-drug-resistant HIV, and should remain an important therapeutic consideration. Data Availability Statement: The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author.","The paper states that data are available on request, but does not confirm that the sequences were made publicly available."
41023944,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024. According to DREAM protocols, individuals with virological failure were enrolled in a six-month enhanced adherence support program; no ART regimen changes are made during the enhanced adherence support phase. A repeated viral load test was performed after this period, and those with persistent viremia (>1000 copies/mL) were eligible for genotypic resistance testing. Only individuals on DTG-containing regimens at the time of resistance testing were included in this analysis.","The paper discusses genotypic resistance testing performed on HIV-positive individuals, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, specifically those experiencing virological failure on DTG-based ART. The inclusion of resistance mutations and the use of the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database further confirm the sequencing of HIV from patient samples."
41023944,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"This study reports the presence of DTG resistance and multiclass failure in Mozambique, suggesting potential limitations of current strategies. Sentinel surveillance and expanded access to resistance testing are important to preserve the efficacy of DTG-based regimens and inform future deployment of long-acting therapies in sub-Saharan Africa.",The paper focuses on genotypic resistance testing and the prevalence of resistance mutations in patient samples rather than in vitro drug susceptibility testing.
41023944,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No GenBank accession numbers were provided,"""The datasets used and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.""","The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that they were not provided."
41023944,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Integrase,"Resistance mutations were identified in the integrase region, and, when available, in the reverse transcriptase and protease regions. Sequencing of the HIV integrase region was performed using the Sanger method at INQABA Biotechnical Industries, South Africa.","The paper specifically mentions the sequencing of the HIV integrase region, along with reverse transcriptase and protease regions when available, during resistance testing."
41023944,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Mozambique,"We conducted a retrospective observational study in five DREAM centers in Mozambique. The DREAM program is a community-based, primary care HIV initiative that integrates clinical follow-up, laboratory monitoring, and social support, and has been active in Mozambique since 2002.","The study was conducted in five DREAM centers in Mozambique, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from this country."
41023944,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2023-2024,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024. We included HIV-positive individuals on DTG-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) with confirmed virological failure (HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL) between July 2022 and December 2024 were included.","The samples were obtained from participants experiencing virological failure between 2023 and 2024, as the study aimed to assess resistance during this period."
41023944,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Sequencing of the HIV integrase region was performed using the Sanger method at INQABA Biotechnical Industries, South Africa. Resistance mutations were identified via Sanger sequencing and interpreted using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database.",The paper explicitly states that Sanger sequencing was used to determine resistance mutations in the HIV integrase region.
41023944,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024. According to DREAM protocols, individuals with virological failure were enrolled in a six-month enhanced adherence support program; no ART regimen changes are made during the enhanced adherence support phase. A repeated viral load test was performed after this period, and those with persistent viremia (>1000 copies/mL) were eligible for genotypic resistance testing. Only individuals on DTG-containing regimens at the time of resistance testing were included in this analysis.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, focusing instead on direct sequencing of the viral RNA."
41023944,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Plasma samples from participants with confirmed virological failure were collected and sent to the Zimpeto DREAM laboratory, where viral RNA was extracted and amplified using an in-house reverse transcription and nested PCR method.",The paper specifies that plasma samples were collected from participants with confirmed virological failure for resistance testing.
41023944,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024. Among them, 13 (46.4%) had evidence of resistance to DTG, while 15 (53.6%) remained susceptible.","The study specifically included individuals on DTG-based ART who experienced virological failure, indicating that sequences were obtained from these individuals."
41023944,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This is a retrospective observational study conducted across five centers of the DREAM program in Mozambique, including two centers in Maputo City, one in Maputo Province, and two in Sofala Province. The current analysis builds on a previously published cohort, expanding the sample by including all eligible participants who underwent genotypic resistance testing due to confirmed virological failure, even after an adherence reinforce intervention, while on DTG-based ART between July 2022 and December 2024. Inclusion criteria were: (i) confirmed HIV-positive status; (ii) being on a DTG-based regimen at the time of resistance testing; (iii) confirmed virological failure (HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL) after at least six months of enhanced adherence support. Exclusion criteria included: (i) switching from DTG to another regimen before resistance testing; (ii) insufficient plasma samples for sequencing; or (iii) incomplete clinical records.","The paper describes a retrospective observational study rather than a clinical trial, focusing on real-world data from DREAM centers."
41023944,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,28,"In the considered period, 28 individuals on DTG-based ART who underwent resistance testing following confirmed virological failure (HIV RNA 0510 copies/mL) after six months of enhanced adherence support were included in the analysis.","The study included 28 individuals on DTG-based ART who underwent resistance testing, indicating the number of samples obtained for sequencing."
41023944,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024. All DTG-resistant individuals who underwent testing for other drug classes (n = 9) also showed co-resistance to NRTIs and/or NNRTIs.","The paper mentions including individuals on DTG-based ART who had experienced virological failure, and had also received other drugs (NRTI and NNRTI), indicating the sequences were from individuals who had previously received antiretroviral drugs."
41023944,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,NRTI and NNRTI,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024. All DTG-resistant individuals who underwent testing for other drug classes (n = 9) also showed co-resistance to NRTIs and/or NNRTIs.","The paper mentions that individuals had resistance to NRTI and/or NNRTI, indicating they had received drugs from these classes before resistance testing."
41023944,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Emtricitabine, Abacavir, Lamivudine, Zidovudine, Nevirapine, Etravirine, Rilpivirine, Lopinavir, Ritonavir, Didanosine, Efavirenz, Saquinavir","We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024. All DTG-resistant individuals who underwent testing for other drug classes (n = 9) also showed co-resistance to NRTIs and/or NNRTIs. Inclusion criteria were: (i) confirmed HIV-positive status; (ii) being on a DTG-based regimen at the time of resistance testing; (iii) confirmed virological failure (HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL) after at least six months of enhanced adherence support. Exclusion criteria included: (i) switching from DTG to another regimen before resistance testing; (ii) insufficient plasma samples for sequencing; or (iii) incomplete clinical records. Resistance to DTG was classified using Stanford scoring.","The paper lists the individuals received drugs from NRTI (Tenofovir, Abacavir, Lamivudine, Zidovudine, Didanosine, Emtricitabine), NNRTI (Nevirapine, Etravirine, Efavirenz, Rilpivirine), PI (Lopinavir, Ritonavir), INSTI (Dolutegravir), indicating individuals were on regimens comprising these drugs before sample sequencing."
41023944,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The datasets used and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.,"The paper states that the datasets used during the study are not publicly available, indicating that the sequences were not made publicly accessible."
41029850,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"This study describes patterns of acquired drug resistance (ADR) and potentially active drugs for subsequent ART regimens in individuals failing treatment in Cameroon. We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the 'Chantal Biya' International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon. Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing and analysed using Stanford HIVdatabase.v.9.4.","The study mentions sequencing individual samples with confirmed virological failure, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported."
41029850,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"This study describes patterns of acquired drug resistance (ADR) and potentially active drugs for subsequent ART regimens in individuals failing treatment in Cameroon. We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the 'Chantal Biya' International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon. Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing and analysed using Stanford HIVdatabase.v.9.4.","The paper focuses on genotypic resistance testing and patterns of drug resistance, rather than providing in vitro susceptibility data."
41029850,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"PQ893197, PQ893192, OQ985810, OR259747, PQ893191","sequences have been submitted to Genebank with available accession numbers provided (PQ893197, PQ893192, OQ985810, OR259747 and PQ893191)",The paper explicitly lists the GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
41029850,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Protease and reverse-transcriptase genes,Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing.,"The study mentions sequencing the protease and reverse-transcriptase genes, indicating these are the genes reported."
41029850,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Cameroon,This study describes patterns of acquired drug resistance (ADR) and potentially active drugs for subsequent ART regimens in individuals failing treatment in Cameroon.,"The study was conducted at the 'Chantal Biya' International Reference Centre in Yaoundé, Cameroon, indicating the country of origin for the sequenced samples."
41029850,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022-2023,"Individuals received for GRT were consecutively enrolled at CIRCB, between January 2022 to April 2023.","The enrollment period for the samples was between January 2022 to April 2023, indicating the years from which the samples were obtained."
41029850,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing.,The study specifies that Sanger sequencing was used for the genotypic resistance testing.
41029850,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,The study does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It focuses on the use of Sanger sequencing directly on the individual samples.,The absence of any mention of cloning indicates that the samples were not cloned before sequencing.
41029850,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma viral load samples,"GRT was performed in the pol gene (protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT)) on the stored samples with plasma viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml, using the previously validated inhouse protocol as described by Fokam et al. [21].",The study mentioned that GRT was performed on the samples with plasma viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml.
41029850,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the 'Chantal Biya' International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon. GRT is recommended following confirmed virological failure to second-line ARTs (viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml), in children experiencing failure under protease inhibitors (PI/r), adolescents with multiple treatment changes and clinical suspicion of HIVDR (that is high viral load despite adherence). Data collection was done using a pre-established form from patients' files. Variables included socio-demographics, ART and immunovirological history (viral load and CD4 count), WHO clinical stages, date of HIV-1 infection, starting date of ART and ART durations.",The paper states the study was conducted among ART-failing individuals and the GRT was recommended following confirmed virological failure.
41029850,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"A cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study was conducted during the reporting period of March to April 2023 at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre for research on HIV/AIDS prevention and management (CIRCB) in Yaoundé-Cameroon among ART-failing PWH. Participants were on documented ART for at least 12 months and experiencing virological failure (viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml). N.B This study may be initiated in patients who present for GRT with received antiretrovirals (ARV) that are listed in Table 1 en 2. Enrolment procedure and data collection. Individuals received for GRT were consecutively enrolled at CIRCB, between January 2022 to April 2023. In the public health approach, GRT is recommended following confirmed virological failure to second-line ARTs (viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml), in children experiencing failure under protease inhibitors (PI/r), adolescents with multiple treatment changes and clinical suspicion of HIVDR (that is high viral load despite adherence).","The study was conducted as a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study, not a clinical trial."
41029850,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,203,"Of 2109 individuals assessed, 203 met inclusion criteria.","The study states that out of 2109 individuals assessed, 203 met the inclusion criteria and had samples for HIV sequencing."
41029850,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,Participants were on documented ART for at least 12 months and experiencing virological failure (viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml).,"The study mentions that participants were on documented ART for at least 12 months, indicating they had previously received ARV drugs."
41029850,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI/r, INSTI","Details on first-line, second-line and third-line regimens are provided. First-line regimens: INST + 2NRTI (preferentially TDF + 3TC + DTG) or NRTI + 2NRTI (AZT + 3TC + EFV/NVP). Second-line regimens: PI/r (ATV/r or LPV/r) plus optimized NRTI backbone. Third-line regimens: regimens containing either DTG and/or DRV/r and/or NRTI.","The paper provides detailed information on the regimens received, which include NRTI, NNRTI, PI/r, and INSTI drug classes."
41029850,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"ABC, 3TC, EFV, NVP, AZT, TDF, DTG, ATV, LPV, DRV","Details on first-line, second-line and third-line regimens are provided. First-line regimens: ABC + 3TC + EFV/NVP (0.98%); AZT + 3TC + EFV/NVP (1.47) TDF + 3TC + EFV/NVP (6.89%); TDF + 3TC + DTG (7.39%%); AZT + 3TC + DTG (0.49%); ABC + 3TC + DTG (3.94%); Second-line regimens: ATV/r + optimized backbone (7/9) on ZDV + 3TC + LPV/r (5.42%); Third-line regimens: TDF + 3TC + LPV/r (3.94%).",The paper lists the specific drugs included in the treatment regimens received by the participants.
41029850,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"sequences have been submitted to Genebank with available accession numbers provided (PQ893197, PQ893192, OQ985810, OR259747 and PQ893191)","The paper mentions the sequences have been submitted to Genebank, indicating they were made publicly available."
41056006,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Eligible participants were treatment naïve, ≥18 years old, and had screening plasma HIV-1 RNA of ≥200 copies/mL and CD4 cell counts of ≥200 cells/μL without active hepatitis B or C infection. Calibrate was a phase 2, randomized, open-label, active-controlled, multicenter study. Treatment-naive PwH were enrolled across 41 sites in the USA and Dominican Republic.",The paper does not mention any sequencing of HIV samples from patients. It focuses on the efficacy and safety of lenacapavir-based regimens in treatment-naive individuals without reporting specific HIV sequences from patient samples.
41056006,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"CALIBRATE was a Phase 2, randomized, open-label study (NCT04143594). Treatment-naive PWH were randomized (2:2:2:1) to four treatment groups (TG): TG1 and TG2 received twice-yearly subcutaneous lenacapavir plus oral daily emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide (F/TAF); TG3 received oral daily lenacapavir plus F/TAF; and TG4 received oral daily bictegravir/F/TAF. At W28, TG1 switched to lenacapavir plus TAF and TG2 switched to lenacapavir plus bictegravir. At W80, TG4 completed the study and TG1-3 continued assigned treatments.",The paper discusses clinical outcomes and resistance data but does not provide any in vitro drug susceptibility testing results. The focus is on the clinical efficacy of the treatment regimens.
41056006,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,None,"Emergence of LEN resistance was an exploratory endpoint. HIV-1 capsid genotypic and phenotypic data were assessed at study entry using the GenoSure Gag-Pro and PhenoSense Gag-Pro assays (Monogram Biosciences). Resistance testing was performed for any participants who met one of the following virologic failure criteria: suboptimal virological response, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL and a decline of <1log10 from Day 1 to Week 10; virological rebound, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL after reaching <50 copies/mL, or two consecutive visits with an increase from nadir of >1log10; or participants with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL at last study visit.",The paper does not mention any specific GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates. It mentions the GenoSure Gag-Pro and PhenoSense Gag-Pro assay but does not provide specific data on GenBank accession numbers.
41056006,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,None,"Emergence of LEN resistance was an exploratory endpoint. HIV-1 capsid genotypic and phenotypic data were assessed at study entry using the GenoSure Gag-Pro and PhenoSense Gag-Pro assays (Monogram Biosciences). Resistance testing was performed for any participants who met one of the following virologic failure criteria: suboptimal virological response, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL and a decline of <1log10 from Day 1 to Week 10; virological rebound, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL after reaching <50 copies/mL, or two consecutive visits with an increase from nadir of >1log10; or participants with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL at last study visit.",The paper mentions the sequencing of the capsid gene but does not provide specific details on other HIV genes that were sequenced.
41056006,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,USA and Dominican Republic,"This study was approved by the institutional review board or ethics committee at all sites and was conducted in compliance with international laws and guidelines. Eligible participants were treatment naïve, ≥18 years old, and had screening plasma HIV-1 RNA of ≥200 copies/mL and CD4 cell counts of ≥200 cells/μL without active hepatitis B or C infection. Calibrate was a phase 2, randomized, open-label, active-controlled, multicenter study. Treatment-naive PwH were enrolled across 41 sites in the USA and Dominican Republic",The paper mentions that treatment-naive individuals were enrolled across 41 sites in the USA and the Dominican Republic.
41056006,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019 to 2020,"Between 22 November 2019 and 27 August 2020, 183 participants in CALIBRATE were randomly assigned to one of four TGs.",The paper states that participants were enrolled between 22 November 2019 and 27 August 2020.
41056006,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Not reported,"Emergence of LEN resistance was an exploratory endpoint. HIV-1 capsid genotypic and phenotypic data were assessed at study entry using the GenoSure Gag-Pro and PhenoSense Gag-Pro assays (Monogram Biosciences). Resistance testing was performed for any participants who met one of the following virologic failure criteria: suboptimal virological response, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL and a decline of <1log10 from Day 1 to Week 10; virological rebound, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL after reaching <50 copies/mL, or two consecutive visits with an increase from nadir of >1log10; or participants with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL at last study visit.","The paper doesn't mention any specific sequencing method. It focuses on the treatment regimen and its outcomes, but details on the sequencing method used are not provided."
41056006,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,Not reported,"Emergence of LEN resistance was an exploratory endpoint. HIV-1 capsid genotypic and phenotypic data were assessed at study entry using the GenoSure Gag-Pro and PhenoSense Gag-Pro assays (Monogram Biosciences). Resistance testing was performed for any participants who met one of the following virologic failure criteria: suboptimal virological response, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL and a decline of <1log10 from Day 1 to Week 10; virological rebound, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL after reaching <50 copies/mL, or two consecutive visits with an increase from nadir of >1log10; or participants with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL at last study visit.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It focuses on resistance and genotypic data but does not provide details on the cloning of samples.
41056006,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Eligible participants were treatment naïve, ≥18 years old, and had screening plasma HIV-1 RNA of ≥200 copies/mL and CD4 cell counts of ≥200 cells/μL without active hepatitis B or C infection. All participants provided written informed consent.","The paper mentions that eligible participants had screening plasma HIV-1 RNA of ≥200 copies/mL, indicating that plasma samples were sequenced."
41056006,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,,,The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen. It focuses on treatment-naive participants and the emergence of resistance mutations.
41056006,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"CALIBRATE was a Phase 2, randomized, open-label study (NCT04143594). Treatment-naive PWH were randomized (2:2:2:1) to four treatment groups (TG): TG1 and TG2 received twice-yearly subcutaneous lenacapavir plus oral daily emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide (F/TAF); TG3 received oral daily lenacapavir plus F/TAF; and TG4 received oral daily bictegravir/F/TAF. At W28, TG1 switched to lenacapavir plus TAF and TG2 switched to lenacapavir plus bictegravir. At W80, TG4 completed the study and TG1-3 continued assigned treatments.",The paper describes a Phase 2 clinical trial (CALIBRATE) conducted to assess the efficacy and safety of lenacapavir-based regimens.
41056006,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"The paper does not report any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing, even though it mentions genotypic and phenotypic data.",The paper does not mention any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing.
41056006,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"Eligible participants were treatment naïve, ≥18 years old, and had screening plasma HIV-1 RNA of ≥200 copies/mL and CD4 cell counts of ≥200 cells/μL without active hepatitis B or C infection. All participants provided written informed consent.   Treatment-naive studies are essential for evaluating the efficacy of new drugs; this is the first study to evaluate LEN in that context. It was designed to generate exploratory clinical data to assist the future development of LEN-containing regimens and provide supportive safety data for the approval of LEN for HTE PWH, not necessarily to test specific antiretroviral therapy combinations for evaluation in future Phase 3 trials.","The paper focuses on treatment-naive individuals, and the clinical trial conducted on the patient was meant to test a new drug, lenacapavir, and its efficacy and safety, not the sequences."
41056006,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"Eligible participants were treatment naïve, ≥18 years old, and had screening plasma HIV-1 RNA of ≥200 copies/mL and CD4 cell counts of ≥200 cells/μL without active hepatitis B or C infection. All participants provided written informed consent.   Treatment-naive studies are essential for evaluating the efficacy of new drugs; this is the first study to evaluate LEN in that context. It was designed to generate exploratory clinical data to assist the future development of LEN-containing regimens and provide supportive safety data for the approval of LEN for HTE PWH, not necessarily to test specific antiretroviral therapy combinations for evaluation in future Phase 3 trials.","The paper focuses on treatment-naive individuals, and the clinical trial conducted on the patient was meant to test a new drug, lenacapavir, and its efficacy and safety, not the sequences."
41056006,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"Eligible participants were treatment naïve, ≥18 years old, and had screening plasma HIV-1 RNA of ≥200 copies/mL and CD4 cell counts of ≥200 cells/μL without active hepatitis B or C infection. All participants provided written informed consent.   Treatment-naive studies are essential for evaluating the efficacy of new drugs; this is the first study to evaluate LEN in that context. It was designed to generate exploratory clinical data to assist the future development of LEN-containing regimens and provide supportive safety data for the approval of LEN for HTE PWH, not necessarily to test specific antiretroviral therapy combinations for evaluation in future Phase 3 trials.","The paper focuses on treatment-naive individuals, and the clinical trial conducted on the patient was meant to test a new drug, lenacapavir, and its efficacy and safety, not the sequences."
41056006,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"This study was approved by the institutional review board or ethics committee at all sites and was conducted in compliance with international laws and guidelines. Eligible participants were treatment naïve, ≥18 years old, and had screening plasma HIV-1 RNA of ≥200 copies/mL and CD4 cell counts of ≥200 cells/μL without active hepatitis B or C infection. All participants provided written informed consent.   Emergence of LEN resistance was an exploratory endpoint. HIV-1 capsid genotypic and phenotypic data were assessed at study entry using the GenoSure Gag-Pro and PhenoSense Gag-Pro assays (Monogram Biosciences). Resistance testing was performed for any participants who met one of the following virologic failure criteria: suboptimal virological response, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL and a decline of <1log10 from Day 1 to Week 10; virological rebound, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL after reaching <50 copies/mL, or two consecutive visits with an increase from nadir of >1log10; or participants with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL at last study visit.","The paper doesn't mention any public sequences, and its focus on resistance in the participants showing failure conditions."
41057785,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Method This cohort study retrospectively reviewed medical records of 235 People living with HIV (PLHIV) who underwent HIV drug resistance testing at the Mildmay-Uganda Hospital from 2018 to 2023.,This cohort study retrospectively reviewed medical records of 235 People living with HIV (PLHIV) who underwent HIV drug resistance testing at the Mildmay-Uganda Hospital from 2018 to 2023.,"The paper mentions that HIV drug resistance testing was conducted on patient samples, indicating that sequences were obtained from patients."
41057785,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data.
41057785,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No evidence,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced isolates.
41057785,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,No evidence,No evidence,The paper does not specify which genes were sequenced.
41057785,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Uganda,"This study was conducted at MUgH, a centre of excellence for HIV care and treatment in peri-urban Wakiso District, serving ∼15,000 PLHIV.","The study was conducted at Mildmay-Uganda Hospital, and all samples were from Uganda."
41057785,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018 to 2023,"This study examined DRM prevalence and their association with time to virological failure, informing strategies to optimize HIV treatment outcomes. Methods This cohort study retrospectively reviewed medical records of 235 People living with HIV (PLHIV) who underwent HIV drug resistance testing at the Mildmay-Uganda Hospital from 2018 to 2023. Data on demographics, ART, and DRMs were analyzed. A cohort study retrospectively reviewed medical records of People living with HIV-1 who underwent HIV drug resistance testing at the Mildmay Uganda Hospital (MUgH) from 2018 to 2023.",The study mentions that the samples were collected from 2018 to 2023.
41057785,7,What method was used for sequencing?,No evidence,No evidence,The paper does not specify the exact sequencing method used.
41057785,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
41057785,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,No evidence,No evidence,The paper does not specify the type of clinical samples sequenced.
41057785,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"most DRM testing followed second-line failure (189, 80.4%), mainly on NRTI + PI (78, 40.6%)",The paper mentions that the study involved participants experiencing virological failure.
41057785,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,This cohort study retrospectively reviewed medical records of People living with HIV-1 who underwent HIV drug resistance testing at the Mildmay Uganda Hospital (MUgH) from 2018 to 2023.,The study is a retrospective cohort review and not a clinical trial.
41057785,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,235,"Data were collected from April to June 2024, including HIV DRM results, socio-demographic, and medical history from ART registers, patient cards, Electronic Medical Records (EMR), and the National HIVDR database [12]. Data were entered in Excel, exported to Stata, and verified against hard copies. Table 1 summarises data for 235 PLHIV, with a mean age of 28.0 (SD = 15.0) and ART duration of 11.2 years (SD = 5.2).",The paper states that 235 People living with HIV underwent HIV drug resistance testing.
41057785,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"most DRM testing followed second-line failure (189, 80.4%), mainly on NRTI + PI (78, 40.6%)","The paper mentions that most DRM testing was done for individuals failing on second-line regimens, indicating prior ARV exposure."
41057785,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","Table 2 Prevalence of drug resistance mutations across ART regimen drug classes. DRMs were detected in 140 of 235 participants (59.6%). NNRTI mutations were most prevalent at 52.8% (124/235; 95% CI: 46.4-59.2), followed by NRTI mutations at 50.2% (118/235; 95% CI: 43.8-56.6). PI major and accessory mutations were less frequent, 25.5% (60/235; 95% CI: 20.0-31.1) and 4.3% (10/235; 95% CI: 1.7-6.8), respectively. INSTI mutations were rare: 0.9% major (2/235; 95% CI: 0.3-2.0) and 0.4% accessory (1/235; 95% CI: 0.4-1.3) as shown in Table 2 below.",The paper mentions that mutations were detected in all classes of drugs received.
41057785,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"AZT, TDF, D4T, ABC, LPV/r, EFV, NVP","Table 1 Demographic and clinical characteristics. Most were male (123, 52.3%), underweight (81, 43.3%) and had a last viral load between 10,000 and 100,000 copies before DRM testing (108, 47.6%). Most PLHIV were initiated on NNRTIs as the anchor drug (126, 54.8%), whereas the most initiated backbone was AZT(Zidovudine)-based (93, 40.8%).","The paper lists the drugs that were part of the regimens, indicating which drugs were received before sequencing."
41057785,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No evidence,No evidence,The paper does not mention any public availability of the sequences.
41088231,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"The NFLG sequence of strain 230794 was 8260 nucleotides (nt) in length, with two CRF02_AG segments substituting in the backbone of subtype F2 in the vif and env gene regions.","The NFLG sequence of strain 230794 was 8260 nucleotides (nt) in length, with two CRF02_AG segments substituting in the backbone of subtype F2 in the vif and env gene regions.","The paper discusses the sequencing of HIV-1 from a patient, specifically mentioning the near full-length genome (NFLG) of HIV-1 obtained from strain 230794 isolated from an individual in Guangdong, China. This indicates that the study involved HIV sequences from patient samples."
41088231,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No drug-resistant mutation was identified, but an INSTI other mutation, L74l, was identified.","No drug-resistant mutation was identified, but an INSTI other mutation, L74l, was identified.","While the paper discusses drug resistance mutations, it does not mention any in vitro susceptibility testing or data. The focus is on genotypic analysis and not on phenotypic (in vitro) drug susceptibility experiments."
41088231,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,PV187006,"The NFLG sequence of 230794, which was 8260 nucleotides (nt) in length, was obtained and submitted to GenBank (accession number PV187006).",The paper explicitly states that the NFLG sequence of strain 230794 was submitted to GenBank with the accession number PV187006.
41088231,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Near full-length genome (NFLG), partial pol gene, vif gene, env gene.",The near full-length genome (NFLG) of HIV-1 was amplified with two overlapping segments by the near endpoint dilution method; we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen; the vif and env gene regions.,"The paper mentions sequencing the near full-length genome (NFLG) of HIV-1, and also mentions sequencing the partial pol gene, vif gene and env gene regions."
41088231,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"Here, we observed a URF composed of subtypes F2 and CRF02_AG isolated from a man (230794) in Guangdong, China. Specimen 230794 was collected in Oct. 2023 from a 47-year-old man who claimed to have contracted HIV-1 through heterosexual contact in Guangzhou City, Guangdong Province, China.","The paper clearly states that the HIV-1 strain 230794 was isolated from a patient in Guangdong, China."
41088231,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2023,"In the previous drug resistance surveillance among 5602 HIV-1-infected antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naive individuals in Guangdong Province from Jan. 2023 to Dec. 2023, we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen collected from an HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG ; The 2025 whole blood sample was collected in 2025.","The paper provides a timeline for the sample collection, indicating 2023 for the plasma sample and 2025 for the whole-blood sample."
41088231,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Positive products were directly purified and sequenced by Sangon Biotech (Shanghai) Co., Ltd with Sanger sequencing method.",The paper mentions that positive PCR products were directly purified and sequenced by Sangon Biotech using the Sanger sequencing method.
41088231,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Positive products were directly purified and sequenced by Sangon Biotech (Shanghai) Co., Ltd with Sanger sequencing method.","The paper states that positive products were directly purified and sequenced, which means no samples were cloned prior to sequencing."
41088231,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,"Plasma, whole blood","Viral RNA was extracted from the 2023 plasma sample using a magnetic bead-based method. Proviral DNA was extracted from the whole-blood sample collected in 2025 with the Viral DNA Extraction Kit (Supbio, China).","The paper specifies that RNA was extracted from a plasma sample and DNA was extracted from a whole-blood sample, indicating that both plasma and whole blood were sequenced."
41088231,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen collected from an HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG, two subtypes of HIV that are very rare in China and even worldwide. Specimen 230794 was collected in Oct. 2023 from a 47-year-old man who claimed to have contracted HIV-1 through heterosexual contact in Guangzhou City, Guangdong Province, China.  The baseline HIV-1 viral load was 3.95 × 105 IU/mL, and the CD4+ T-cell count was 77 cells/μL. These values indicated that this patient was in the advanced stage of HIV-1 infection, which had not been identified and treated in a timely manner, resulting in severe immune system damage.","The paper describes the patient 230794 as untreated advanced stage of HIV-1 infection which was identified as a suspected recombinant subtype, indicating the HIV-1 infection did not receive any treatment and hadn't got virological failure on a treatment regimen."
41088231,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This study was approved by the institutional review board of Guangzhou Eighth People's Hospital (No. 202353290), and the individual provided written informed consent. In the previous drug resistance surveillance among 5602 HIV-1-infected antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naive individuals in Guangdong Province from Jan. 2023 to Dec. 2023, we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen collected from an HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG, two subtypes of HIV that are very rare in China and even worldwide.","The paper does not mention that the patient was part of a clinical trial. It states that the study was approved by an institutional review board and that the patient provided informed consent, which is standard for ethical compliance but does not indicate a clinical trial."
41088231,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,One,"In the previous drug resistance surveillance among 5602 HIV-1-infected antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naive individuals in Guangdong Province from Jan. 2023 to Dec. 2023, we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen collected from an HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG.","The paper mentions that one partial pol gene sequence was identified from one HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG."
41088231,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"In the previous drug resistance surveillance among 5602 HIV-1-infected antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naive individuals in Guangdong Province from Jan. 2023 to Dec. 2023, we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen collected from an HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG, two subtypes of HIV that are very rare in China and even worldwide. Specimen 230794was collected in Oct. 2023 from a 47-year-old man who claimed to have contracted HIV-1 through heterosexual contact in Guangzhou City, Guangdong Province, China.","The paper states that the individual was  identified from a drug resistance surveillance among 5602 ART-naïve individuals in 2023, indicating the individual had not received ARV drugs."
