41093949,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"No, the paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples.",The HIV-1 CRF01_AE and subtype B Pol region sequences were downloaded from the HIV Sequence Database. No HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples,The study utilized existing sequences from the HIV Sequence Database rather than reporting new sequences from patient samples.
41093949,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"Yes, the paper reports in vitro drug susceptibility data.",In vitro comparison of the susceptibilities of the same drug resistance mutations to reverse-transcriptase inhibitors of subtype B and CRF01_AE HIV-1 strains,"The paper details phenotypic resistance tests and IC50 values for various drugs, indicating in vitro testing of drug susceptibilities."
41093949,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates are PQ735970 to PQ735985.,The NCBI repository accession numbers for the AE/B recombinants are: PQ735970-PQ735985.,The paper explicitly lists these accession numbers in the results section.
41093949,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,The Pol region of HIV-1 was reported to have been sequenced.,The HIV-1 CRF01_AE and subtype B Pol region sequences were downloaded from the HIV Sequence Database.,The paper mentions sequencing the Pol region for the CRF01_AE and subtype B sequences.
41093949,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained from China and the United States.,"According to the following criteria: (1) from database creation to December 31,2023; (2) country labeled as CN (China) or US (United States);",The paper specifies that sequences were obtained from these two countries based on the criteria for selection.
41093949,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained from 1978 to 2023.,"A total of 8654 subtype B sequences (76.36% from the USA) and 10,551 CRF01_AE sequences (0.07% from the USA) were obtained after sorting and were sampled from 1978 to 2023.",The paper states the time frame during which the samples were obtained.
41093949,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing was used for sequencing.,"Positive clones were picked and amplified in LB broth containing ampicillin, followed by Sanger sequencing (by SinoGenoMax).",The paper mentions that positive clones were sequenced using Sanger sequencing.
41093949,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,"Yes, samples were cloned prior to sequencing.","The mutants were transformed into Stabl3 competent cells (TransGen, Cat. No. CD521-02) and inoculated on LB agar plates containing ampicillin (100μg/ml) (TransGen, Cat. No. GG101-01) for amplification. Positive clones were picked and amplified in LB broth containing ampicillin, followed by Sanger sequencing.","The paper describes the process of transforming mutants into competent cells and picking positive clones for sequencing, indicating cloning was done."
41093949,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasmid samples containing the HIV genome were sequenced.,"The wild-type infectious cloning plasmid pNL4-3 of HIV-1 subtype B was kindly provided by Professor Shan Lu of the University of Massachusetts Medical School, USA. the infectious cloning plasmid pGX002 of CRF01_AE is a full-length genomic infectious clone of the HIV-1 CRF01_AE strain","The paper discusses sequencing HIV-1 infectious cloning plasmids, which are not clinical samples."
41093949,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"No, the paper does not mention obtaining sequences from individuals with virological failure.","The HIV-1 CRF01_AE and subtype B Pol region sequences were downloaded from the HIV Sequence Database (https://www.hiv.lanl.gov/) according to the following criteria: (1) from database creation to December 31,2023; (2) country labeled as CN (China) or US (United States); (3) complete information (geographic location, year the sequence was sampled, earliest sequence taken from same subject) available; and (4) contained the Pol region. The genotype resistance analysis was performed by uploading the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database (https://hivdb.stanford.edu/) after trimming with BioEdit v7.2.5 software alignment. In this study, the top five drug resistance sites were selected through a combination of the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database and its drug resistance interpretation tool. A single site of the circulating RTI DRMs was introduced into HIV-1 infectious cloning plasmids by site-directed mutagenesis for in vitro phenotypic drug susceptibility testing.","The paper does not mention individuals with virological failure, it mentions samples were cloned from infectious clones."
41093949,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"No, the paper does not mention that patients were in a clinical trial.","The HIV-1 CRF01_AE and subtype B Pol region sequences were downloaded from the HIV Sequence Database (https://www.hiv.lanl.gov/) according to the following criteria: (1) from database creation to December 31,2023; (2) country labeled as CN (China) or US (United States); (3) complete information (geographic location, year the sequence was sampled, earliest sequence taken from same subject) available; and (4) contained the Pol region.",The paper does not mention if patients were in a clinical trial.
41093949,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,The paper does not specify the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing.,The HIV-1 CRF01_AE and subtype B Pol region sequences were downloaded from the HIV Sequence Database.,"The paper does not mention how many individuals had samples made, it only mentions the samples were sequenced."
41093949,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"No, the paper does not report sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.","The HIV-1 CRF01_AE and subtype B Pol region sequences were downloaded from the HIV Sequence Database (https://www.hiv.lanl.gov/) according to the following criteria: (1) from database creation to December 31,2023; (2) country labeled as CN (China) or US (United States); (3) complete information (geographic location, year the sequence was sampled, earliest sequence taken from same subject) available; and (4) contained the Pol region.",The paper does not mention if any individuals had received previous ARV drugs.
41093949,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,The paper does not report which drug classes were received by individuals before sample sequencing.,"The HIV-1 CRF01_AE and subtype B Pol region sequences were downloaded from the HIV Sequence Database (https://www.hiv.lanl.gov/) according to the following criteria: (1) from database creation to December 31,2023; (2) country labeled as CN (China) or US (United States); (3) complete information (geographic location, year the sequence was sampled, earliest sequence taken from same subject) available; and (4) contained the Pol region.",The paper does not mention if any individuals had received previous ARV drugs.
41093949,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,The paper does not report which drugs were received by individuals before sample sequencing.,"The HIV-1 CRF01_AE and subtype B Pol region sequences were downloaded from the HIV Sequence Database (https://www.hiv.lanl.gov/) according to the following criteria: (1) from database creation to December 31,2023; (2) country labeled as CN (China) or US (United States); (3) complete information (geographic location, year the sequence was sampled, earliest sequence taken from same subject) available; and (4) contained the Pol region.",The paper does not mention if any individuals had received previous ARV drugs.
41093949,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,"Yes, sequences from the paper were made publicly available.","The sequences generated during the current study are available in the NCBI repository, PQ735970-PQ735985.",The paper explicitly states that the sequences are publicly available in the NCBI repository.
41112839,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"A total of 237 samples from different patients were analyzed, and 131 (55.3%) were successfully amplified and sequenced.","A total of 237 samples from different patients were analyzed, and 131 (55.3%) were successfully amplified and sequenced.","The paper mentions that samples from different patients were analyzed and sequenced, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported."
41112839,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No in vitro drug susceptibility data were reported.,No in vitro drug susceptibility data were reported.,The study focused on genotyping and drug resistance mutations rather than in vitro susceptibility testing.
41112839,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,PP280627 to PP280757,The RT-generated sequences were deposited in the National Institutes of Health GenBank database under accession numbers PP280627 to PP280757.,The paper explicitly states that the sequences were deposited in GenBank under these accession numbers.
41112839,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Partial reverse transcriptase (RT) gene,Amplification and sequencing were performed on the partial reverse transcriptase (RT) gene using the Agence Nationale de Recherches sur le Sida et les hepatitis virales (ANRS: French National Agency for Research on acquired immunodeficiency syndrome [AIDS] and Viral Hepatitis)/AC11 method at the HIV National Reference Laboratory.,The paper mentions the sequencing of the partial reverse transcriptase (RT) gene.
41112839,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Senegal,"This study aimed to document the prevalence of pretreatment drug resistance in antiretroviral therapy-naive patients in Senegal, describe mutation profiles, and evaluate their potential impact on future therapy.",The study was conducted in Senegal and focused on patients from 35 sites throughout the country.
41112839,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017 to 2018,PDR surveillance was carried out between 2017 and 2018 according to WHO recommendations.,The paper states that the samples were collected between 2017 and 2018.
41112839,7,What method was used for sequencing?,"Big Dye Terminator v3.1 (Life Technologies, Courtaboeuf, France)","Purified and quantified polymerase chain reaction products were sequenced by using Big Dye Terminator v3.1 (Life Technologies, Courtaboeuf, France), and electrophoresis was performed on the ABI 3100 Avant sequencer (Applied Biosystems, France) as previously described.",The paper describes the use of Big Dye Terminator v3.1 for sequencing.
41112839,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.,The study does not indicate that cloning was performed before sequencing.
41112839,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Dried blood spots (DBS) and whole blood samples,"In Dakar (capital city), sample collection involved taking venous blood samples on ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) tubes from consenting patients. These samples were transported with an ice pack to the HIV National Reference Laboratory (NRL) of the Bacteriology-Virology laboratory at University Hospital Center (UHC) Aristide Le Dantec, where they were centrifuged at 2500rpm to recover the plasma, which was stored at -80C until testing. In other sites outside Dakar, venous blood samples on EDTA tubes were used to prepare 2 DBS cards by saturating dedicated circles of Whatman 903 paper with whole blood. These blood spots were dried overnight or for at least 3 hours at room temperature and preserved with desiccants at 20 - 37C on site before being transferred within 15 days to the reference laboratory in Dakar.",The paper mentions the use of both dried blood spots (DBS) and whole blood samples.
41112839,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"The inclusion criteria for patients in the sites were to be HIV-1-positive men or women, aged at least 18, ART-naive (defined as never having received any antiretroviral therapy, including prevention of mother-to-child transmission [PMTCT] regimens), and newly diagnosed based on the patient and the healthcare provider declarations. A total of 251 of 331 ART-naive patients were recruited, giving a participation rate of (75.8%). Drug resistance surveillance is mandatory for any HIV treatment program as recommended by WHO due to the risk of the emergence.","The paper included only antiretroviral therapy-naive individuals, and the patients were newly diagnosed, indicating that they had not experienced virological failure."
41112839,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,The study does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial.,The paper describes a survey study rather than a clinical trial.
41112839,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,A total of 237 samples from different patients were analyzed,"A total of 237 samples from different patients were analyzed, and 131 (55.3%) were successfully amplified and sequenced.",The paper states that 237 samples from different patients were analyzed.
41112839,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"The inclusion criteria for patients in the sites were to be HIV-1-positive men or women, aged at least 18, ART-naive (defined as never having received any antiretroviral therapy, including prevention of mother-to-child transmission [PMTCT] regimens), and newly diagnosed based on the patient and the healthcare provider declarations. A total of 251 of 331 ART-naive patients were recruited, giving a participation rate of (75.8%). Drug resistance surveillance is mandatory for any HIV treatment program as recommended by WHO due to the risk of the emergence.","The paper included only antiretroviral therapy-naive individuals, and the patients were newly diagnosed, indicating that they had not previously received ARV drugs."
41112839,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"The inclusion criteria for patients in the sites were to be HIV-1-positive men or women, aged at least 18, ART-naive (defined as never having received any antiretroviral therapy, including prevention of mother-to-child transmission [PMTCT] regimens), and newly diagnosed based on the patient and the healthcare provider declarations. A total of 251 of 331 ART-naive patients were recruited, giving a participation rate of (75.8%). Drug resistance surveillance is mandatory for any HIV treatment program as recommended by WHO due to the risk of the emergence.","The paper included only antiretroviral therapy-naive individuals, and the patients were newly diagnosed, indicating that they had not received any antiretroviral drugs."
41112839,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"The inclusion criteria for patients in the sites were to be HIV-1-positive men or women, aged at least 18, ART-naive (defined as never having received any antiretroviral therapy, including prevention of mother-to-child transmission [PMTCT] regimens), and newly diagnosed based on the patient and the healthcare provider declarations. A total of 251 of 331 ART-naive patients were recruited, giving a participation rate of (75.8%). Drug resistance surveillance is mandatory for any HIV treatment program as recommended by WHO due to the risk of the emergence.","The paper included only antiretroviral therapy-naive individuals, and the patients were newly diagnosed, indicating that they had not received any antiretroviral drugs."
41112839,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,The RT-generated sequences were deposited in the National Institutes of Health GenBank database under accession numbers PP280627 to PP280757.,The paper explicitly states that the sequences were deposited in GenBank under these accession numbers.
41129268,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Data for this analysis were obtained following the 2020 revision of the Uganda HIV Prevention, Care and Treatment Guidelines that recommended HIVDR testing for all children and adolescents living with HIV failing ART. HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) and drug susceptibility profiles were generated using Stanford University's HIVdb algorithm (version 9.0) (https://hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb).","The paper reports on the analysis of drug resistance mutations and drug susceptibility profiles generated from patient samples, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from these samples."
41129268,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The analytic dataset of demographic data and HIVDR information for sampled specimens were retrieved from the HIVDR Database in March 2022. HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) and drug susceptibility profiles were generated using Stanford University's HIVdb algorithm (version 9.0) (https://hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb). All analyses were conducted using Stata 18 statistical software (StataCorp LLC, College Station, TX). Frequencies of DRMs were calculated overall and stratified by drug class of the mutation, including mutations against NRTIs, NNRTIs, protease inhibitors (PIs), and integrase inhibitors (INSTIs). The frequencies of individual DRMs were also calculated overall and stratified by regimen at sample collection. Mutations and resistance were classified as non-NNRTI if they were identified in any of the following drug classes; NRTIs, PIs, and/or INSTIs. Individuals were considered to have clinically significant HIVDR if their drug susceptibility profile revealed intermediate-to-high level drug resistance to one or more of the following individual ARVs: DTG, abacavir (ABC), zidovudine (AZT), tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF), lamivudine (3TC), LPV/r, darunavir (DRV), EFV, or NVP. All calculations were estimated overall, and stratified by regimen at blood sample collection, and by previous exposure to the child's known historical treatment regimens.","The paper discusses the generation of drug susceptibility profiles using algorithms, but it does not mention conducting in vitro assays to determine drug susceptibility."
41129268,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that this information is not available."
41129268,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Not reported,"Low Levels of Clinically Significant Drug Resistance Mutations to Dolutegravir amongst Children Living with HIV Failing on First-line Antiretroviral Therapy in Uganda. We identified CLHIV aged 0-15 years experiencing virologic failure on first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Uganda between January-November 2021 from the Central Public Health Laboratory database. Remnant viral load samples were tested for HIVDR using Sanger sequencing. Drug resistance mutations (DRMs) were identified and antiretroviral susceptibility was interpreted using the Stanford University HIVDR Database algorithms. Prevalence of DRMs and HIVDR were summarized using descriptive statistics, and associations with demographic and clinical factors were determined using chi-squared tests.","The paper focuses on drug resistance mutations and susceptibility profiles, but it does not specify which HIV genes were sequenced."
41129268,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Uganda,"Data for this analysis were obtained following the 2020 revision of the Uganda HIV Prevention, Care and Treatment Guidelines that recommended HIVDR testing for all children and adolescents living with HIV failing ART. In 2021, the Uganda Ministry of Health conducted standard-of-care HIVDR testing for CLHIV failing first-line ART to optimize treatment outcomes, through the support of the CDC-funded Faith-based Action for Scaling up Testing and Treatment for the Epidemic Response (FASTER) project. FASTER was implemented between 2019-2021 by Catholic Relief Services, in partnership with the Clinton Health Access Initiative, to support ministries of health in four countries (Uganda, Nigeria, Tanzania and Zambia) to improve testing and treatment of CLHIV. We identified CLHIV aged 0-15 years experiencing virologic failure on first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Uganda between January-November 2021 from the Central Public Health Laboratory database.",The paper clearly states that the study was conducted in Uganda and the samples were obtained from this country.
41129268,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2021,"We identified CLHIV aged 0-15 years experiencing virologic failure on first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Uganda between January-November 2021 from the Central Public Health Laboratory database. Between January and November 2021, 648 VL samples from 164 sites met the inclusion criteria.",The paper mentions that the samples were obtained between January and November 2021.
41129268,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"The eligible VL samples were then automatically identified by the HIV Drug Resistance Database (HIVDR Database). The HIVDR Database acts as the relay database for cross-laboratory systems interoperability. The database received eligible sample profiles from the VL LIMS and provided a user interface for referring these samples to the HIVDR laboratories. Once a sample fulfilling the inclusion criteria above was identified, it was retrieved and sent to the HIVDR laboratory at Joint Clinical Research Centre to be tested using Sanger sequencing technology if there was sufficient sample volume. Once a sample was referred to the HIVDR laboratory, clinicians received email notifications from the HIVDR Database to upload their clients' social, psychosocial, and medical profiles into the database. When the HIVDR results were available, the HIVDR laboratory systems automatically pushed the results into the HIVDR Database, from which clinicians and experts could review the results and make ART recommendations. Once individual treatment decisions were made and documented, these clinical recommendations were also uploaded into the HIVDR database.",Paper explicitly states that Sanger sequencing was used to test the samples for HIVDR.
41129268,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The eligible VL samples were then automatically identified by the HIV Drug Resistance Database (HIVDR Database). The HIVDR Database acts as the relay database for cross-laboratory systems interoperability. The database received eligible sample profiles from the VL LIMS and provided a user interface for referring these samples to the HIVDR laboratories. Once a sample fulfilling the inclusion criteria above was identified, it was retrieved and sent to the HIVDR laboratory at Joint Clinical Research Centre to be tested using Sanger sequencing technology if there was sufficient sample volume. Once a sample was referred to the HIVDR laboratory, clinicians received email notifications from the HIVDR Database to upload their clients' social, psychosocial, and medical profiles into the database. When the HIVDR results were available, the HIVDR laboratory systems automatically pushed the results into the HIVDR Database, from which clinicians and experts could review the results and make ART recommendations. Once individual treatment decisions were made and documented, these clinical recommendations were also uploaded into the HIVDR database.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, indicating that this method was not used."
41129268,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Viral load samples,"We identified CLHIV aged 0-15 years experiencing virologic failure on first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Uganda between January-November 2021 from the Central Public Health Laboratory database. Remnant viral load samples were tested for HIVDR using Sanger sequencing. Data for this analysis came from previously collected VL samples from children aged ≤15 years of age on first-line ART enrolled in any ART-accredited health facility in Uganda whose VL sample satisfied the following criteria: (1) sample tested for VL between January and November 2021, (2) sample type was plasma. The eligible VL samples were then automatically identified by the HIV Drug Resistance Database (HIVDR Database). The HIVDR Database acts as the relay database for cross-laboratory systems interoperability. The database received eligible sample profiles from the VL LIMS and provided a user interface for referring these samples to the HIVDR laboratories. Once a sample fulfilling the inclusion criteria above was identified, it was retrieved and sent to the HIVDR laboratory at Joint Clinical Research Centre to be tested using Sanger sequencing technology if there was sufficient sample volume. Once a sample was referred to the HIVDR laboratory, clinicians received email notifications from the HIVDR Database to upload their clients' social, psychosocial, and medical profiles into the database. When the HIVDR results were available, the HIVDR laboratory systems automatically pushed the results into the HIVDR Database, from which clinicians and experts could review the results and make ART recommendations. Once individual treatment decisions were made and documented, these clinical recommendations were also uploaded into the HIVDR database.","The paper specifies that remnant viral load cfrom samples were used, indicating that these were the type of specimens sequenced."
41129268,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"he HIVDR Database acts as the relay database for cross-laboratory systems interoperability. The database received eligible sample profiles from the VL LIMS and provided a user interface for referring these samples to the HIVDR laboratories. We identified CLHIV aged 0-15 years experiencing virologic failure on first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Uganda between January-November 2021 from the Central Public Health Laboratory database. Samples from CLHIV whose prior regimen changes were made for treatment simplification or due to policy changes and not VF were classified as being on first-line ART. The potential clinical recommendations included maintaining on current regimen, substituting with another ARV within the first-line, switching to another drug class or second-line regimen, or pending if clinical recommendations were not noted. At the time of data collection, almost half of CLHIV in the database (N=145, 43%) had no information on clinical recommendations after HIVDR testing. However, among those with clinical recommendations, 94 (28.2%) were maintained on their current regimen, 55 (16.5%) were switched to another drug class, and 39 (11.7%) had a single drug substitution. This analysis demonstrated low levels of INSTI and PI HIVDR among Ugandan CLHIV experiencing VF on first-line ART and represents the largest examination of HIVDR among CLHIV on DTG-based regimens in Sub-Saharan Africa to date. While overall rates of DRMs were high, the majority of DRMs were to NNRTIs with low rates to INSTIs and PIs. We found that CLHIV on a DTG-based regimen were least likely to have any DRMs (including non-NNRTI DRMs) compared to all other regimens, with >95% susceptible to DTG, LPV/r, and DRV/r. The finding of limited DTG resistance amongst children failing on DTG indicates that most children could be maintained on their DTG-based ART with more emphasis on intensified, effective adherence support to help them achieve VS. Low rates of DRV/r resistance further support the use of DRV as an effective second-line ARV, even amongst those CLHIV failing on LPV/r.","The paper focuses on children living with HIV experiencing virologic failure on first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART), indicating that sequences were obtained from these individuals."
41129268,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Data for this analysis were obtained following the 2020 revision of the Uganda HIV Prevention, Care and Treatment Guidelines that recommended HIVDR testing for all children and adolescents living with HIV failing ART. In 2021, the Uganda Ministry of Health conducted standard-of-care HIVDR testing for CLHIV failing first-line ART to optimize treatment outcomes, through the support of the CDC-funded Faith-based Action for Scaling up Testing and Treatment for the Epidemic Response (FASTER) project. FASTER was implemented between 2019-2021 by Catholic Relief Services, in partnership with the Clinton Health Access Initiative, to support ministries of health in four countries (Uganda, Nigeria, Tanzania and Zambia) to improve testing and treatment of CLHIV. We identified CLHIV aged 0-15 years experiencing virologic failure on first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Uganda between January-November 2021 from the Central Public Health Laboratory database. Samples from CLHIV whose prior regimen changes were made for treatment simplification or due to policy changes and not VF were classified as being on first-line ART. The potential clinical recommendations included maintaining on current regimen, substituting with another ARV within the first-line, switching to another drug class or second-line regimen, or pending if clinical recommendations were not noted. At the time of data collection, almost half of CLHIV in the database (N=145, 43%) had no information on clinical recommendations after HIVDR testing. However, among those with clinical recommendations, 94 (28.2%) were maintained on their current regimen, 55 (16.5%) were switched to another drug class, and 39 (11.7%) had a single drug substitution.",The paper describes a routine standard-of-care HIVDR testing rather than a clinical trial setting.
41129268,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,333,"Among 333 successfully genotyped patient samples, 122 (36.6%) CLHIV were on a DTG-based regimen and 136 (40.8%) on a lopinavir-based regimen. Among all CLHIV, 271 (81.4%) had DRMs and 262 (78.7%) had any intermediate-to-high level HIVDR, (including DTG (1.8%), lopinavir (9.6%), abacavir (ABC) (25.5%) and zidovudine (19.2%)). Between January and November 2021, 648 VL samples from 164 sites met the inclusion criteria. A total of 428 (66.0%) samples were retrieved from the sample biorepository and sent to the HIVDR lab for testing; 128 (19.8%) did not meet the Sanger testing criteria due to low sample volume or other factors. Of the 428 samples referred for HIVDR testing, 333 (77.8%) samples were successfully tested while 95 (22.2%) samples failed to amplify.","The paper states that 333 samples were successfully genotyped , indicating the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained."
41129268,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Samples from CLHIV whose prior regimen changes were made for treatment simplification or due to policy changes and not VF were classified as being on first-line ART. At the time of sample collection, 122 (36.6%) CLHIV were taking a DTG-based regimen, 136 (40.8%) were taking an LPV/r-based regimen, and 74 (22.2%) were taking either an EFV- or NVP-based regimen. Among the 122 CLHIV on DTG-based regimens, the median age was 13 years (interquartile range [IQR]=11-14); 54 (44.2%) were on regimens with an ABC/3TC backbone, 65 (53.3%) had a TDF/3TC backbone, and 3 (2.5%) had an AZT/3TC backbone. Data for this analysis came from previously collected VL samples from children aged ≤15 years of age on first-line ART enrolled in any ART-accredited health facility in Uganda whose VL sample satisfied the following criteria: (1) sample tested for VL between January and November 2021, (2) sample type was plasma. We identified CLHIV aged 0-15 years experiencing virologic failure on first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Uganda between January-November 2021 from the Central Public Health Laboratory database. Remnant viral load samples were tested for HIVDR using Sanger sequencing. Data for this analysis were obtained following the 2020 revision of the Uganda HIV Prevention, Care and Treatment Guidelines that recommended HIVDR testing for all children and adolescents living with HIV failing ART. In 2021, the Uganda Ministry of Health conducted standard-of-care HIVDR testing for CLHIV failing first-line ART to optimize treatment outcomes, through the support of the CDC-funded Faith-based Action for Scaling up Testing and Treatment for the Epidemic Response (FASTER) project.","The paper mentions the collection of samples from CLHIV whose prior regimen changes were made for treatment simplification or due to policy changes, indicating that these samples were from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
41129268,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs","At the time of sample collection, 122 (36.6%) CLHIV were taking a DTG-based regimen, 136 (40.8%) were taking an LPV/r-based regimen, and 74 (22.2%) were taking either an EFV- or NVP-based regimen. Among the 122 CLHIV on DTG-based regimens, the median age was 13 years (interquartile range [IQR]=11-14); 54 (44.2%) were on regimens with an ABC/3TC backbone, 65 (53.3%) had a TDF/3TC backbone, and 3 (2.5%) had an AZT/3TC backbone. Data for this analysis came from previously collected VL samples from children aged ≤15 years of age on first-line ART enrolled in any ART-accredited health facility in Uganda whose VL sample satisfied the following criteria: (1) sample tested for VL between January and November 2021, (2) sample type was plasma. We identified CLHIV aged 0-15 years experiencing virologic failure on first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Uganda between January-November 2021 from the Central Public Health Laboratory database. Remnant viral load samples were tested for HIVDR using Sanger sequencing. Samples from CLHIV whose prior regimen changes were made for treatment simplification or due to policy changes and not VF were classified as being on first-line ART. Data for this analysis were obtained following the 2020 revision of the Uganda HIV Prevention, Care and Treatment Guidelines that recommended HIVDR testing for all children and adolescents living with HIV failing ART. In 2021, the Uganda Ministry of Health conducted standard-of-care HIVDR testing for CLHIV failing first-line ART to optimize treatment outcomes, through the support of the CDC-funded Faith-based Action for Scaling up Testing and Treatment for the Epidemic Response (FASTER) project. FASTER was implemented between 2019-2021 by Catholic Relief Services, in partnership with the Clinton Health Access Initiative, to support ministries of health in four countries (Uganda, Nigeria, Tanzania and Zambia) to improve testing and treatment of CLHIV.","The paper lists the specific drug regimens the participants were on at the time of collection, which include drugs from the NRTI, NNRTI, PI, and INSTI classes."
41129268,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"DTG, Lopinavir, Atazanavir, Raltegravir, Abacavir, Zidovudine, Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Efavirenz, Nevirapine","At the time of sample collection, 122 (36.6%) CLHIV were taking a DTG-based regimen, 136 (40.8%) were taking an LPV/r-based regimen, and 74 (22.2%) were taking either an EFV- or NVP-based regimen. Among the 122 CLHIV on DTG-based regimens, the median age was 13 years (interquartile range [IQR]=11-14); 54 (44.2%) were on regimens with an ABC/3TC backbone, 65 (53.3%) had a TDF/3TC backbone, and 3 (2.5%) had an AZT/3TC backbone. Data for this analysis came from previously collected VL samples from children aged ≤15 years of age on first-line ART enrolled in any ART-accredited health facility in Uganda whose VL sample satisfied the following criteria: (1) sample tested for VL between January and November 2021, (2) sample type was plasma. We identified CLHIV aged 0-15 years experiencing virologic failure on first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Uganda between January-November 2021 from the Central Public Health Laboratory database. Remnant viral load samples were tested for HIVDR using Sanger sequencing. Samples from CLHIV whose prior regimen changes were made for treatment simplification or due to policy changes and not VF were classified as being on first-line ART. Data for this analysis were obtained following the 2020 revision of the Uganda HIV Prevention, Care and Treatment Guidelines that recommended HIVDR testing for all children and adolescents living with HIV failing ART. In 2021, the Uganda Ministry of Health conducted standard-of-care HIVDR testing for CLHIV failing first-line ART to optimize treatment outcomes, through the support of the CDC-funded Faith-based Action for Scaling up Testing and Treatment for the Epidemic Response (FASTER) project. FASTER was implemented between 2019-2021 by Catholic Relief Services, in partnership with the Clinton Health Access Initiative, to support ministries of health in four countries (Uganda, Nigeria, Tanzania and Zambia) to improve testing and treatment of CLHIV.","The paper lists the specific drugs included in the regimens that participants were on, which include DTG, Lopinavir, Atazanavir, Raltegravir, Abacavir, Zidovudine, Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Efavirenz, and Nevirapine."
41129268,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The analysis took place before full introduction of DTG 10mg in Uganda so further investigation into this age band is warranted as most CLHIV <5 years will have now fully transitioned to DTG. The data presented in this work were therefore a combination of the routine data inputs by clinicians, VL and HIVDR laboratories, and drug resistance review committees, all aggregated within the HIVDR Database. These data were stored in a relational database and were extracted, cleaned, and transformed using the Structured Query Language and exported as a Comma Separated Value database for further cleaning and analysis. The HIVDR Database acts as the relay database for cross-laboratory systems interoperability. The database received eligible sample profiles from the VL LIMS and provided a user interface for referring these samples to the HIVDR laboratories. Once a sample fulfilling the inclusion criteria above was identified, it was retrieved and sent to the HIVDR laboratory at Joint Clinical Research Centre to be tested using Sanger sequencing technology if there was sufficient sample volume.","The paper does not mention that the sequences were made publicly available, and the access to data was very limited."
41130593,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Participants who acquired HIV-1 with a viral load of ≥ 200 copies/mL were evaluated for resistance by genotyping of the HIV-1 capsid, protease, reverse transcriptase and integrase genes at HIV diagnosis.","The paper discusses the sequencing of HIV-1 genes from patient samples for resistance analysis, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
41130593,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,Conclusions. Acquisition of HIV while receiving LEN and resistance to LEN in the context of unrecognized HIV was rare but associated with the emergence of the N74D LEN resistance-associated substitution in this population.,The paper focuses on in vivo resistance data from clinical trials and does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results.
41130593,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not Applicable,No evidence,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that this information was not reported."
41130593,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"HIV-1 capsid, protease, reverse transcriptase and integrase","Participants who acquired HIV-1 with a viral load of ≥ 200 copies/mL were evaluated for resistance by genotyping of the HIV-1 capsid, protease, reverse transcriptase and integrase genes at HIV diagnosis.","The paper explicitly mentions that the HIV-1 capsid, protease, reverse transcriptase and integrase genes were sequenced for resistance analysis."
