41130593,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Emtricitabine, tenofovir disoproxil fumarate, dolutegravir","Participants who acquired HIV-1 with a viral load of ≥ 200 copies/mL were evaluated for resistance by genotyping of the HIV-1 capsid, protease, reverse transcriptase and integrase genes at HIV diagnosis 28. All sequences were aligned to the HIV HXB2 reference sequence using MAFFT 24. All sequences were aligned to the HIV HXB2 reference sequence using MAFFT 24.",The paper discusses the drugs that were part of the treatment regimens.
41130593,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences, indicating that this was not done."
41140464,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"No, the study employed a meta-analysis approach to evaluate the subtype distribution, diversity and drug resistance of the HIV-1 pol gene across EA. Data on HIV-1 pol gene sequences were retrieved from public database. Using appertaining software, the sequences were analyzed for assessment of DRMs in the pol gene enzymes (PR, RT, and IN).","In June 2025, HIV-1 pol gene sequences deposited between 2015 and 2025 from four East African countries were retrieved in FASTA format with subtyping information from the Los Alamos National Laboratory (LANL) HIV Database. The sequences were sourced from four EA countries: Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, and Ethiopia.","The paper reports HIV-1 pol gene sequences retrieved from public databases, not directly from patient samples."
41140464,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The Genetic Diversity and Drug Resistance Patterns of HIV-1 Pol Gene in East Africa Human immunodeficiency virus-1 (HIV-1) is among the most genetically diverse pathogens due to expeditious molecular evolution. The rapid change in HIV genomes intrigates HIV transmission and progression and attributes HIV resistance to antiretroviral therapy (ART). In East Africa, as in other parts of the globe, HIV-1 occurs in various subtypes, circulating recombinant form (CRF) and unique recombinant forms, with subtype A1 being the most predominant. Surveillance of HIV-1 molecular diversity and drug resistance mutations (DRMs) is a linchpin for monitoring viral evolution and treatment efficiency. However, consolidated reports on the same are limited, and therefore, the pursuit of meta-analysis was sought to analyze genetic diversity and drug resistance patterns of HIV-1 pol gene and their geographical distributions in four East African countries (Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, and Ethiopia). We retrieved 7614 HIV-1 pol gene sequences, deposited between 2015 and 2025 from the Los Alamos HIV databases. The predominant HIV-1 subtypes were A1 (40.2%), C (21.5%), and D (17.7%), with geographical variability. A notable frequency of inter-subtype recombinant was observed with recombinants A1D (9.5%) and A1C (2.94%) being prevalent. Few CRFs (>0.1%) were identified. DRM were present in 42.8% of the sequences, with the majority associated with NNRTIs (36.5%) and NRTIs (25.5%). The most frequently associated mutations were K103N and M184V. Although resistance to INSTI (3.7%) remained minimal, its presence warrants continued monitoring. A significant association between HIV-1 subtypes and DRM prevalence was observed (𝜒2=102.43,p<0.0001), with subtypes showing varied resistance burdens. These findings underscore the variability in HIV-1 genetic diversity across studied East African countries, highlighting the need for region-specific interventions, to optimize HIV-1 control in this region.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility assays or results.
41140464,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"Data on HIV-1 pol gene sequences were retrieved from public database. Using appertaining software, the sequences were analyzed for assessment of DRMs in the pol gene enzymes (PR, RT, and IN). 2767 sequences from Kenya, 2673 sequences from Uganda, 1811 sequences from Tanzania, and 1090 sequences from Ethiopia.","A total of 7724 HIV-1 pol gene sequences were initially retrieved from LANL HIV sequence database, comprising sequence collected and deposited between 2015 and 2025. Following quality filtering which involved the removal of duplicate entries and sequences with high levels of ambiguous nucleotide characters, 110 sequences were excluded, resulting in 7614 sequences for downstream analysis. These included 5730 PR/RT, 734 RT, and 1594 IN sequences. The sequences were sourced from four EA countries: Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, and Ethiopia. The distribution of HIV-1 pol gene sequences by country is presented in Table 1.  The sequence data retrieved from LANL had been subjected to quality filtering using pre-approprofile bioinformatic software, including removal of duplicate entries and elimination of sequences exhibiting high levels of ambiguous nucleotide characters (>3% total IUPAC ambiguity codes), ensuring that only sequences of sufficient quality and clinical relevance were utilized in the final analyses. Unique sequences with valid subtyping information in FASTA format were used in downstream analyses after quality control, and were analyzed for the presence of drug resistance mutations. Similarly, a high level of nucleotide ambiguity (>3%) leads to exclusion of mutated sequences whereby previous publications with absence of this data or sequenceing quality checking would not exclude these ambiguous sequences yielding to biased resistance estimates.  Thus, the presence of replicates of sequences which was also not mentioned in previous publications is an additional limitation to interpretation of the results. In addition, sequences with more than 3% IUPAC nucleotide ambiguity codes (e.g., M, R, Y) across the full pol gene were excluded from the dataset. The dataset may be skewed toward viruses with DRMs, limiting the generalizability of the results to the broader HIV-positive population, and also the inability to retrieve detailed metadata on treatment history, which prevent differentiation between transmitted and acquired DRMs which is an important distinction for interpreting resistance patterns.",The paper does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
41140464,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease, Reverse Transcriptase, Integrase","Using appertaining software, the sequences were analyzed for assessment of DRMs in the pol gene enzymes (PR, RT, and IN). These included 5730 PR/RT, 734 RT, and 1594 IN sequences.","The study analyzed sequences from the protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase regions of the pol gene."
41140464,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, Ethiopia","The sequences were sourced from four EA countries: Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, and Ethiopia.","The paper states that the sequences were retrieved from four East African countries: Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, and Ethiopia."
41140464,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2015-2025,"Data on HIV-1 pol gene sequences were retrieved from public database. Using appertaining software, the sequences were analyzed for assessment of DRMs in the pol gene enzymes (PR, RT, and IN). In June 2025, HIV-1 pol gene sequences deposited between 2015 and 2025 from four East African countries were retrieved in FASTA format with subtyping information from the Los Alamos National Laboratory (LANL) HIV Database. A total of 7724 HIV-1 pol gene sequences were initially retrieved from LANL HIV sequence database, comprising sequence collected and deposited between 2015 and 2025. TABLE 1: The HIV-1 pol gene sequence from East Africa retrieved from public database by June 2025.",The paper mentions that the sequences were deposited between 2015 and 2025.
